": "Beta-glucan (oats) + anthocyanins (blueberries) independently blunt glucose absorption; together the effect is additive; the combination is one of the most evidence-backed breakfast choices for metabolic health"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Brain health synergy", "Mechanism": "DHA (salmon) provides structural substrate for neuronal membranes while anthocyanins (blueberries) reduce neuroinflammation and stimulate BDNF \u2014 synergistic for neuroplasticity and cognitive function"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Post-training recovery pair", "Mechanism": "Anthocyanins reduce exercise-induced oxidative stress; egg protein and leucine drive muscle protein synthesis \u2014 a complementary recovery combination"}]}], "calories": "57", "protein": "0.7g", "carbs": "14.5g", "sugar": "9.96g", "fat": "0.3g", "sat._fat": "0g", "fibre": "2.4g", "cholesterol": "0mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Amount": "80g", "Cal": "46", "Carbs": "11.6g", "Sugar": "8.0g", "Fibre": "1.9g", "Vitamin C": "7.8mg", "Anthocyanins": "~132mg"}, {"Amount": "100g", "Cal": "57", "Carbs": "14.5g", "Sugar": "9.96g", "Fibre": "2.4g", "Vitamin C": "9.7mg", "Anthocyanins": "~165mg"}, {"Amount": "150g", "Cal": "86", "Carbs": "21.8g", "Sugar": "14.9g", "Fibre": "3.6g", "Vitamin C": "14.6mg", "Anthocyanins": "~248mg"}], "minerals_breakdown": [{"Mineral": "Manganese", "Per 100g": "0.3mg", "% RDA": "13\u201315%", "Role": "Mitochondrial SOD2 antioxidant enzyme cofactor"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "77mg", "% RDA": "2%", "Role": "Electrolyte balance"}, {"Mineral": "Copper", "Per 100g": "0.06mg", "% RDA": "7%", "Role": "SOD1 cofactor; iron metabolism"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "0.3mg", "% RDA": "2\u20134%", "Role": "Oxygen transport"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "6mg", "% RDA": "1%", "Role": "ATP synthesis (minimal contribution)"}], "vitamins_breakdown": [{"Vitamin": "Vitamin K", "Per 100g": "19\u00b5g", "% RDA": "16\u201323%", "Role": "Bone mineralisation; coagulation"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin C", "Per 100g": "9.7mg", "% RDA": "11%", "Role": "Antioxidant; collagen synthesis; iron absorption"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin E", "Per 100g": "0.6mg", "% RDA": "4%", "Role": "Fat-soluble antioxidant"}, {"Vitamin": "Folate", "Per 100g": "6\u00b5g", "% RDA": "2%", "Role": "DNA synthesis; methylation"}], "food_synergies": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Fat-soluble phytonutrient absorption", "Mechanism": "Avocado''s monounsaturated fat significantly increases bioavailability of blueberries'' fat-soluble polyphenols (beta-carotene, lutein); the absorption of polyphenols generally improves substantially when co-ingested with dietary fat"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]", "Synergy": "Double anti-glycaemic effect", "Mechanism": "Beta-glucan (oats) + anthocyanins (blueberries) independently blunt glucose absorption; together the effect is additive; the combination is one of the most evidence-backed breakfast choices for metabolic health"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Brain health synergy", "Mechanism": "DHA (salmon) provides structural substrate for neuronal membranes while anthocyanins (blueberries) reduce neuroinflammation and stimulate BDNF \u2014 synergistic for neuroplasticity and cognitive function"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Post-training recovery pair", "Mechanism": "Anthocyanins reduce exercise-induced oxidative stress; egg protein and leucine drive muscle protein synthesis \u2014 a complementary recovery combination"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "fruit", "calories_per_100g": "57", "protein_per_100g": "0.7", "carbs_per_100g": "14.5", "sugar_per_100g": "9.96", "added_sugar": "false", "fat_per_100g": "0.3", "saturated_fat_per_100g": "0.0", "fibre_per_100g": "2.4", "cholesterol_per_100g": "0", "omega3_per_100g_mg": "58", "iron_per_100g": "0.3", "zinc_per_100g": "0.2", "magnesium_per_100g": "6", "potassium_per_100g": "77", "sodium_per_100g": "1", "selenium_per_100g_\u00b5g": "0.1", "choline_per_100g_mg": "6", "vitamin_c_per_100g_mg": "9.7", "vitamin_d_per_100g_iu": "0", "vitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g": "0", "complete_protein": "false", "leucine_per_100g": "0.07", "antioxidant_level": "high", "probiotic": "false", "refined": "false", "seed_oil": "false", "body_systems": "", "serving_unit": "g", "serving_unit_g": "1", "typical_serving": "80g", "typical_serving_g": "80", "glycaemic_index": "53", "mitochondrial_benefit": "high", "callouts": [{"type": "success", "title": "Sports Nutrition Snapshot", "content": "**Anthocyanins: ~165mg / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 53** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\nThe highest-priority fruit in the Good Energy framework \u2014 one of few foods that directly activates mitochondrial biogenesis via PGC-1\u03b1."}, {"type": "quote", "title": "\"Blueberries are the most studied berry for mitochondrial and cognitive health. The anthocyanins cross the blood-brain barrier \u2014 they are feeding your neurons directly.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick", "content": ""}, {"type": "tip", "title": "Anthocyanins \u2014 the primary bioactive compound", "content": "Anthocyanins are water-soluble pigments in the flavonoid family. Blueberries are among the richest dietary sources, and their small molecular size allows them to cross the blood-brain barrier \u2014 a property rare among dietary compounds."}, {"type": "info", "title": "PGC-1\u03b1 and mitochondrial biogenesis", "content": "PGC-1\u03b1 is the master transcription factor for mitochondrial biogenesis \u2014 it signals the cell to make more mitochondria. Anthocyanins from blueberries are one of the few dietary compounds with evidence for directly activating PGC-1\u03b1. This is why blueberries are considered mitochondrially supportive despite being primarily a carbohydrate source."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Optimal range and practical notes", "content": "**Optimal: 80\u2013150g per day**\n- Below 80g: anthocyanin dose falls below levels used in most cognitive and mitochondrial benefit studies\n- 80\u2013150g is the functional range for polyphenol benefit without excessive natural sugar load\n- Above 150g: natural sugars accumulate (9.96g sugar per 100g); diminishing returns on antioxidant benefit per calorie\n\n**Wild blueberries:** 2\u20133\u00d7 higher anthocyanin content than cultivated (smaller berries, more skin per gram, deeper colour). Specifically superior for cognitive and mitochondrial applications if available.\n\n**Frozen = equally nutritious:** blueberries are frozen at peak ripeness, which preserves anthocyanin content. Out-of-season fresh blueberries are often lower in anthocyanins than frozen. No meaningful nutritional reason to prefer fresh unless in season.\n\n**No upper limit concern** for healthy individuals. Blueberries are essentially a free food from a calorie and glycaemic standpoint \u2014 the natural sugar is buffered by fibre and by the anti-glycaemic effect of the anthocyanins themselves."}], "quotes": ["\"Blueberries are the most studied berry for mitochondrial and cognitive health. The anthocyanins cross the blood-brain barrier \u2014 they are feeding your neurons directly.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] Optimal range and practical notes\n> **Optimal: 80\u2013150g per day**\n> - Below 80g: anthocyanin dose falls below levels used in most cognitive and mitochondrial benefit studies\n> - 80\u2013150g is the functional range for polyphenol benefit without excessive natural sugar load\n> - Above 150g: natural sugars accumulate (9.96g sugar per 100g); diminishing returns on antioxidant benefit per calorie\n>\n> **Wild blueberries:** 2\u20133\u00d7 higher anthocyanin content than cultivated (smaller berries, more skin per gram, deeper colour). Specifically superior for cognitive and mitochondrial applications if available.\n>\n> **Frozen = equally nutritious:** blueberries are frozen at peak ripeness, which preserves anthocyanin content. Out-of-season fresh blueberries are often lower in anthocyanins than frozen. No meaningful nutritional reason to prefer fresh unless in season.\n>\n> **No upper limit concern** for healthy individuals. Blueberries are essentially a free food from a calorie and glycaemic standpoint \u2014 the natural sugar is buffered by fibre and by the anti-glycaemic effect of the anthocyanins themselves.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "With oats at breakfast \u00b7 Mixed into Greek yogurt \u00b7 As a post-training snack with eggs \u00b7 Frozen blueberries are equally effective \u2014 often preferred for year-round consistency\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "good_energy_relevance": "Despite containing natural sugars (9.96g/100g), blueberries have a moderate GI (53) and a meaningful anti-glycaemic effect \u2014 their anthocyanins inhibit alpha-glucosidase (the enzyme that breaks down complex carbohydrates into glucose), independently reducing post-meal glucose. Long-term studies show blueberry consumption improves insulin sensitivity. The PGC-1\u03b1 activation is the direct mitochondrial link \u2014 making blueberries one of the few foods that supports all three of Means'' primary targets simultaneously: glucose management, mitochondrial health, and antioxidant protection.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 80g):**\n- Fibre: 1.9g of 50g target (4%)\n- Omega-3: 46mg of 2,000mg target (ALA, trace)\n- Antioxidants: high (anthocyanins, quercetin, chlorogenic acid)\n- Probiotics: none (but polyphenols act as prebiotics \u2014 selectively feeding beneficial gut bacteria)\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Broccoli', 'produce', 55, 3.7, 11.2, 0.6, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "36f4d3051ce6a99306c0f58ef0d0d49f", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/produce\nfood_category: produce\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Produce\nserving_unit: \"1 cup cooked (assumed)\"\ncalories_per_unit: 55\nprotein_per_unit_g: 3.7\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 11.2\nfat_per_unit_g: 0.6\nsugar_per_unit_g: 2\nprocessing_level: \"Whole/Unprocessed\"\nfermented: false\n---\n# Broccoli\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries (no full micronutrient/vitamin breakdown, food synergies, or dosage guidance authored here).\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 cup cooked (assumed) | 55 | 3.7g | 11.2g | 2g | 0.6g |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Produce\n- **Processing Level:** Whole/Unprocessed\n- **Fermented:** No\n\n## Health Benefits\n\nRich in Vitamin C, K, and fiber; may support detoxification.\n\n## Notes\n\nServing size inferred from macros, not explicit in source \u2014 please verify. Good steamed, roasted, or in stir-fries.\n", "portion_state": "cooked", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat"], "rows": [{"Unit": "1 cup cooked (assumed)", "Calories": "55", "Protein": "3.7g", "Carbs": "11.2g", "Sugar": "2g", "Fat": "0.6g"}]}], "unit": "1 cup cooked (assumed)", "calories": "55", "protein": "3.7g", "carbs": "11.2g", "sugar": "2g", "fat": "0.6g", "tags": "", "food_category": "produce", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Produce", "serving_unit": "1 cup cooked (assumed)", "calories_per_unit": "55", "protein_per_unit_g": "3.7", "carbs_per_unit_g": "11.2", "fat_per_unit_g": "0.6", "sugar_per_unit_g": "2", "processing_level": "Whole/Unprocessed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Chia Seeds', 'seed', 486, 16.5, 42.1, 30.7, 34.4, 17830, false, '{"content_hash": "5be887d0a8297cb21093e3105cdccf21", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/seed\nfood_category: seed\n# Macros per 100g (dry)\ncalories_per_100g: 486\nprotein_per_100g: 16.5\ncarbs_per_100g: 42.1\nsugar_per_100g: 0.0\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 30.7\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 3.3\nfibre_per_100g: 34.4\ncholesterol_per_100g: 0\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 17830\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 7.7\nzinc_per_100g: 4.6\nmagnesium_per_100g: 335\npotassium_per_100g: 407\nsodium_per_100g: 16\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 55\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 0\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 1.6\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 0\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: false\nleucine_per_100g: 1.37\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: moderate\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - gut\n  - heart\n  - bones\n  - mitochondria\n  - muscles\n# Serving\nserving_unit: tablespoon\nserving_unit_g: 12\ntypical_serving: 2 tablespoons (24g)\ntypical_serving_g: 24\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 1\nmitochondrial_benefit: high\n---\n# Chia Seeds\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **34.4g fibre / 100g** \u00b7 **17,830mg ALA omega-3 / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 1** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> The highest-fibre food in the vault \u2014 and the most concentrated plant source of omega-3, though conversion to EPA/DHA is limited.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Chia seeds are a remarkable food \u2014 two tablespoons delivers more fibre than most people eat in an entire day, with virtually no glucose impact.\" \u2014 Casey Means\n\nChia seeds are nutritionally extraordinary \u2014 the most concentrated common source of dietary fibre (34.4g per 100g) and a very high source of ALA omega-3. They are also rich in calcium, magnesium, iron, zinc, and selenium. When soaked in liquid, chia seeds form a gel (from the mucilaginous fibre) that slows gastric emptying, blunts glucose absorption, and creates a prolonged satiety effect.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g (dry)\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 486 | 16.5g | 42.1g | 0g | 30.7g | 3.3g | 34.4g | 0mg |\n\n> [!info] Net carbs are very low\n> Total carbs 42.1g \u2212 fibre 34.4g = **7.7g net carbs per 100g**. Despite appearing carbohydrate-dense, chia seeds are effectively a fat and fibre food with negligible glucose impact (GI: 1).\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Fibre | Net Carbs | Omega-3 (ALA) | Magnesium | Calcium |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 tbsp (12g) | 58 | 4.1g | 0.9g | 2,140mg | 40mg | 76mg |\n| 2 tbsp / 24g (typical) | 117 | 8.3g | 1.8g | 4,279mg | 80mg | 151mg |\n| 40g | 194 | 13.8g | 3.1g | 7,132mg | 134mg | 252mg |\n\n> [!tip] Fibre contribution\n> 2 tablespoons (24g) delivers 8.3g fibre \u2014 17% of Casey Means'' 50g/day target in one small addition to any meal.\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Magnesium | 335mg | 80% | ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions; muscle relaxation \u2014 exceptional plant source |\n| Calcium | 631mg | 63% | Bone mineralisation; muscle contraction \u2014 exceptional for a plant food (higher per gram than milk) |\n| Selenium | 55\u00b5g | 100% | Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion; meets full daily requirement in 100g |\n| Iron | 7.7mg | 43\u201396% | Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain \u2014 non-heme; pair with vitamin C for absorption |\n| Zinc | 4.6mg | 42\u201358% | Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function |\n| Phosphorus | 860mg | 69% | ATP production; bone health |\n| Potassium | 407mg | 9% | Electrolyte; muscle contraction |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Niacin (B3) | 8.8mg | 55\u201362% | NAD+ precursor; mitochondrial electron transport |\n| Thiamine (B1) | 0.6mg | 50\u201355% | Glucose-to-energy conversion; gatekeeper enzyme for carbohydrate metabolism |\n| Riboflavin (B2) | 0.17mg | 13% | FAD cofactor; mitochondrial electron transport |\n| Vitamin E | 0.5mg | 3% | Fat-soluble antioxidant (modest) |\n\n---\n\n## Fat and Fibre Profile\n\n### Fat Profile\n\n| Fat Type | Per 100g | Properties |\n|---|---|---|\n| ALA (omega-3) | ~17.8g | Plant-based omega-3; highest of any common food; converts to EPA/DHA at ~5\u201310% efficiency |\n| Linoleic acid (omega-6) | ~5.8g | Omega-6 content is lower relative to omega-3 than almost any plant food; omega-6:omega-3 ratio ~0.3:1 \u2014 exceptional |\n| Palmitic acid (saturated) | ~3.3g | Neutral at this intake level |\n\n> [!warning] ALA vs. EPA/DHA conversion\n> Chia seeds are extraordinarily rich in ALA omega-3, but ALA must be converted to EPA and then DHA to serve the structural and anti-inflammatory functions that Means targets. Most people convert only 5\u201310% of ALA to EPA and <0.5% to DHA. **Chia seeds contribute meaningfully to omega-3 intake but do not replace [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] as the primary EPA/DHA source.**\n\n### Fibre Profile\n\n| Fibre Type | Per 100g | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Soluble (mucilaginous) | ~14\u201316g | Forms a thick gel when wet; slows gastric emptying; dramatically blunts glucose absorption; feeds gut bacteria |\n| Insoluble | ~18\u201320g | Increases stool bulk; feeds different microbial populations |\n| **Total** | **34.4g** | **69% of the Good Energy 50g/day target in 100g \u2014 highest of any common food** |\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] | Synbiotic (prebiotic + probiotic) | Chia''s soluble fibre feeds the live bacterial cultures in Greek yogurt \u2014 creating a highly gut-optimised combination; also dramatically increases satiety of the yogurt |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Fibre amplification | Combining chia and oats in one meal (overnight oats) pushes the total fibre well above 15g per meal \u2014 one of the most effective single-meal gut microbiome inputs |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] | Antioxidant + prebiotic | Blueberry polyphenols and chia fibre together provide both prebiotic substrate and antioxidant signalling to the gut microbiome |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] | Omega-3 complementarity | Chia provides ALA (structural precursor); salmon provides EPA+DHA (active forms) \u2014 together they cover both plant and marine omega-3 pathways |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] Small volume, high impact\n> **Optimal: 2\u20133 tablespoons (24\u201336g) per day**\n> - 2 tablespoons: 8.3g fibre, 4,279mg ALA \u2014 a meaningful daily contribution\n> - 3 tablespoons: 12.4g fibre \u2014 covers ~25% of the 50g/day target alone\n>\n> **Hydration:** chia seeds absorb up to 12\u00d7 their weight in water. Consuming dry chia seeds without adequate liquid can cause an uncomfortable gel mass in the gut. Always soak in liquid for at least 10\u201315 minutes, or mix into yogurt, oats, or a smoothie.\n>\n> **Introducing gradually:** the very high fibre content can cause gas and bloating if intake jumps suddenly. Build up from 1 tablespoon over 1\u20132 weeks.\n>\n> **No upper limit concern** from a toxicity standpoint, but above 4\u20135 tablespoons the calorie contribution (486 cal/100g) becomes significant \u2014 chia is calorie-dense by weight.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nChia seeds are the single most fibre-dense addition to the food library \u2014 2 tablespoons adds 8.3g fibre with almost no glucose impact. The mucilaginous gel directly slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, making it a powerful glucose-blunting addition to any carbohydrate-containing meal. The omega-3:omega-6 ratio (~0.3:1) is the most favourable of any common plant food, actively countering the omega-6 overload that most people carry from seed oil consumption.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 24g / 2 tbsp):**\n- Fibre: 8.3g of 50g target (17%)\n- Omega-3: 4,279mg ALA (converts to ~200\u2013400mg EPA/DHA)\n- Antioxidants: moderate (quercetin, kaempferol, chlorogenic acid)\n- Probiotics: none (but soluble fibre is a highly effective prebiotic)\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nStirred into Greek yogurt overnight \u00b7 Added to oats (overnight chia-oat pudding) \u00b7 Mixed into a smoothie \u00b7 Sprinkled over a salad \u00b7 Soaked in almond milk as chia pudding\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database classified Chia Seeds as **Processing Level: Whole/Unprocessed**, **Fermented: No** \u2014 consistent with this file. Its serving reference was 3 tbsp (30g) = 150 cal / 5g protein / 13g carbs / 9g fat / 11g fibre / 5mg sodium \u2014 a slightly larger serving than the 2 tbsp (24g) typical serving used above, but proportionally consistent with the per-100g figures.\n", "portion_state": "raw_dry", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "486", "Protein": "16.5g", "Carbs": "42.1g", "Sugar": "0g", "Fat": "30.7g", "Sat. Fat": "3.3g", "Fibre": "34.4g", "Cholesterol": "0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Fibre", "Net Carbs", "Omega-3 (ALA)", "Magnesium", "Calcium"], "rows": [{"Amount": "1 tbsp (12g)", "Cal": "58", "Fibre": "4.1g", "Net Carbs": "0.9g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "2,140mg", "Magnesium": "40mg", "Calcium": "76mg"}, {"Amount": "2 tbsp / 24g (typical)", "Cal": "117", "Fibre": "8.3g", "Net Carbs": "1.8g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "4,279mg", "Magnesium": "80mg", "Calcium": "151mg"}, {"Amount": "40g", "Cal": "194", "Fibre": "13.8g", "Net Carbs": "3.1g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "7,132mg", "Magnesium": "134mg", "Calcium": "252mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "335mg", "% RDA": "80%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions; muscle relaxation \u2014 exceptional plant source"}, {"Mineral": "Calcium", "Per 100g": "631mg", "% RDA": "63%", "Role": "Bone mineralisation; muscle contraction \u2014 exceptional for a plant food (higher per gram than milk)"}, {"Mineral": "Selenium", "Per 100g": "55\u00b5g", "% RDA": "100%", "Role": "Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion; meets full daily requirement in 100g"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "7.7mg", "% RDA": "43\u201396%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain \u2014 non-heme; pair with vitamin C for absorption"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "4.6mg", "% RDA": "42\u201358%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "860mg", "% RDA": "69%", "Role": "ATP production; bone health"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "407mg", "% RDA": "9%", "Role": "Electrolyte; muscle contraction"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Niacin (B3)", "Per 100g": "8.8mg", "% RDA": "55\u201362%", "Role": "NAD+ precursor; mitochondrial electron transport"}, {"Vitamin": "Thiamine (B1)", "Per 100g": "0.6mg", "% RDA": "50\u201355%", "Role": "Glucose-to-energy conversion; gatekeeper enzyme for carbohydrate metabolism"}, {"Vitamin": "Riboflavin (B2)", "Per 100g": "0.17mg", "% RDA": "13%", "Role": "FAD cofactor; mitochondrial electron transport"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin E", "Per 100g": "0.5mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "Fat-soluble antioxidant (modest)"}]}, {"headers": ["Fat Type", "Per 100g", "Properties"], "rows": [{"Fat Type": "ALA (omega-3)", "Per 100g": "~17.8g", "Properties": "Plant-based omega-3; highest of any common food; converts to EPA/DHA at ~5\u201310% efficiency"}, {"Fat Type": "Linoleic acid (omega-6)", "Per 100g": "~5.8g", "Properties": "Omega-6 content is lower relative to omega-3 than almost any plant food; omega-6:omega-3 ratio ~0.3:1 \u2014 exceptional"}, {"Fat Type": "Palmitic acid (saturated)", "Per 100g": "~3.3g", "Properties": "Neutral at this intake level"}]}, {"headers": ["Fibre Type", "Per 100g", "Role"], "rows": [{"Fibre Type": "Soluble (mucilaginous)", "Per 100g": "~14\u201316g", "Role": "Forms a thick gel when wet; slows gastric emptying; dramatically blunts glucose absorption; feeds gut bacteria"}, {"Fibre Type": "Insoluble", "Per 100g": "~18\u201320g", "Role": "Increases stool bulk; feeds different microbial populations"}, {"Fibre Type": "**Total**", "Per 100g": "**34.4g**", "Role": "**69% of the Good Energy 50g/day target in 100g \u2014 highest of any common food**"}]}, {"headers": ["Paired With", "Synergy", "Mechanism"], "rows": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]]", "Synergy": "Synbiotic (prebiotic + probiotic)", "Mechanism": "Chia''s soluble fibre feeds the live bacterial cultures in Greek yogurt \u2014 creating a highly gut-optimised combination; also dramatically increases satiety of the yogurt"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]", "Synergy": "Fibre amplification", "Mechanism": "Combining chia and oats in one meal (overnight oats) pushes the total fibre well above 15g per meal \u2014 one of the most effective single-meal gut microbiome inputs"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\