aordinarily rich in ALA omega-3, but ALA must be converted to EPA and then DHA to serve the structural and anti-inflammatory functions that Means targets. Most people convert only 5\u201310% of ALA to EPA and <0.5% to DHA. **Chia seeds contribute meaningfully to omega-3 intake but do not replace [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] as the primary EPA/DHA source.**"}, {"type": "info", "title": "Small volume, high impact", "content": "**Optimal: 2\u20133 tablespoons (24\u201336g) per day**\n- 2 tablespoons: 8.3g fibre, 4,279mg ALA \u2014 a meaningful daily contribution\n- 3 tablespoons: 12.4g fibre \u2014 covers ~25% of the 50g/day target alone\n\n**Hydration:** chia seeds absorb up to 12\u00d7 their weight in water. Consuming dry chia seeds without adequate liquid can cause an uncomfortable gel mass in the gut. Always soak in liquid for at least 10\u201315 minutes, or mix into yogurt, oats, or a smoothie.\n\n**Introducing gradually:** the very high fibre content can cause gas and bloating if intake jumps suddenly. Build up from 1 tablespoon over 1\u20132 weeks.\n\n**No upper limit concern** from a toxicity standpoint, but above 4\u20135 tablespoons the calorie contribution (486 cal/100g) becomes significant \u2014 chia is calorie-dense by weight."}], "quotes": ["\"Chia seeds are a remarkable food \u2014 two tablespoons delivers more fibre than most people eat in an entire day, with virtually no glucose impact.\" \u2014 Casey Means"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] Small volume, high impact\n> **Optimal: 2\u20133 tablespoons (24\u201336g) per day**\n> - 2 tablespoons: 8.3g fibre, 4,279mg ALA \u2014 a meaningful daily contribution\n> - 3 tablespoons: 12.4g fibre \u2014 covers ~25% of the 50g/day target alone\n>\n> **Hydration:** chia seeds absorb up to 12\u00d7 their weight in water. Consuming dry chia seeds without adequate liquid can cause an uncomfortable gel mass in the gut. Always soak in liquid for at least 10\u201315 minutes, or mix into yogurt, oats, or a smoothie.\n>\n> **Introducing gradually:** the very high fibre content can cause gas and bloating if intake jumps suddenly. Build up from 1 tablespoon over 1\u20132 weeks.\n>\n> **No upper limit concern** from a toxicity standpoint, but above 4\u20135 tablespoons the calorie contribution (486 cal/100g) becomes significant \u2014 chia is calorie-dense by weight.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "Stirred into Greek yogurt overnight \u00b7 Added to oats (overnight chia-oat pudding) \u00b7 Mixed into a smoothie \u00b7 Sprinkled over a salad \u00b7 Soaked in almond milk as chia pudding\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Chia seeds are the single most fibre-dense addition to the food library \u2014 2 tablespoons adds 8.3g fibre with almost no glucose impact. The mucilaginous gel directly slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, making it a powerful glucose-blunting addition to any carbohydrate-containing meal. The omega-3:omega-6 ratio (~0.3:1) is the most favourable of any common plant food, actively countering the omega-6 overload that most people carry from seed oil consumption.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 24g / 2 tbsp):**\n- Fibre: 8.3g of 50g target (17%)\n- Omega-3: 4,279mg ALA (converts to ~200\u2013400mg EPA/DHA)\n- Antioxidants: moderate (quercetin, kaempferol, chlorogenic acid)\n- Probiotics: none (but soluble fibre is a highly effective prebiotic)\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Chicken Breast', 'protein', 165, 31.0, 0.0, 3.6, 0.0, 60, false, '{"content_hash": "b9da199ea2e92f1726e0ef70d350ca15", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/protein\nfood_category: protein\n# Macros per 100g (cooked weight)\ncalories_per_100g: 165\nprotein_per_100g: 31.0\ncarbs_per_100g: 0.0\nsugar_per_100g: 0.0\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 3.6\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 1.0\nfibre_per_100g: 0.0\ncholesterol_per_100g: 85\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 60\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 0.7\nzinc_per_100g: 1.0\nmagnesium_per_100g: 29\npotassium_per_100g: 256\nsodium_per_100g: 74\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 27\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 85\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 0\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 4\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0.3\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: true\nleucine_per_100g: 2.47\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: low\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - muscles\n  - mitochondria\n  - immune\n  - hormones\n# Serving\nserving_unit: g\nserving_unit_g: 1\ntypical_serving: 150g cooked\ntypical_serving_g: 150\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 0\nmitochondrial_benefit: high\n---\n# Chicken Breast\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **Complete protein** \u00b7 **2.47g leucine / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 0** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> The highest-leucine common food and the most efficient lean protein for hitting daily targets without overshooting calories.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Lean protein is the foundation of every body composition goal \u2014 it preserves muscle, drives satiety, and costs almost nothing in fat or carbohydrate.\" \u2014 Jeff Nippard\n\nCooked chicken breast delivers over 31g of protein per 100g with minimal fat and zero carbohydrate. It is also the richest common food source of leucine \u2014 the amino acid that directly triggers muscle protein synthesis via the mTOR pathway. Macros below are for **cooked weight**.\n\n> [!warning] Raw vs. Cooked Weight\n> 200g raw chicken breast \u2192 ~150g cooked (roughly 25% water loss during cooking). Always weigh after cooking, or account for the shrinkage when tracking raw weight.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g (cooked)\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 165 | 31.0g | 0g | 0g | 3.6g | 1.0g | 0g | 85mg |\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount (cooked) | Cal | Protein | Fat | Cholesterol | Leucine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 100g | 165 | 31.0g | 3.6g | 85mg | 2.5g |\n| 150g | 248 | 46.5g | 5.4g | 128mg | 3.7g |\n| 200g | 330 | 62.0g | 7.2g | 170mg | 4.9g |\n| 250g | 413 | 77.5g | 9.0g | 213mg | 6.2g |\n\n> [!tip] Leucine at 150g\n> A 150g serving delivers ~3.7g leucine \u2014 well above the ~2.5\u20133g threshold required to maximally stimulate muscle protein synthesis per meal. Chicken breast is one of the most reliable single-food MPS triggers available.\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Selenium | 27\u00b5g | 49% | Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion |\n| Phosphorus | 210mg | 17% | ATP production; bone health |\n| Zinc | 1.0mg | 9\u201313% | Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function |\n| Potassium | 256mg | 5% | Electrolyte balance; muscle contraction |\n| Magnesium | 29mg | 7% | ATP synthesis; muscle relaxation |\n| Iron | 0.7mg | 4\u20139% | Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain |\n| Sodium | 74mg | \u2014 | Fluid balance |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Niacin (B3) | 13.7mg | 86\u201396% | NAD+ precursor \u2014 the essential cofactor for mitochondrial energy production |\n| Vitamin B6 | 0.9mg | 69% | Protein metabolism; neurotransmitter synthesis; glycogen breakdown |\n| Choline | 85mg | 16% | Mitochondrial membrane health; neurotransmitter function |\n| Vitamin B12 | 0.3\u00b5g | 13% | Mitochondrial energy production; nerve function |\n\n> [!info] Niacin (B3) and NAD+\n> Chicken breast is one of the richest dietary sources of niacin \u2014 the precursor to NAD+. NAD+ is the central electron carrier in mitochondrial energy production; declining NAD+ is a primary mechanism of mitochondrial ageing. A 150g serving covers ~90% of the daily niacin requirement.\n\n---\n\n## Amino Acid Profile\n\n> [!tip] Complete Protein \u2014 Highest Common Leucine Source\n> Chicken breast contains all 9 essential amino acids and has the highest leucine concentration of any commonly eaten protein food \u2014 higher than beef, eggs, or salmon on a per-gram basis.\n\n| Amino Acid | Per 100g | Sports Relevance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Leucine | 2.47g | Highest of common foods \u2014 primary mTOR trigger for MPS |\n| Lysine | 2.67g | Collagen synthesis; carnitine production |\n| Isoleucine | 1.43g | BCAA; glucose uptake into muscle during exercise |\n| Valine | 1.49g | BCAA; muscle glycogen sparing |\n| Threonine | 1.35g | Collagen and elastin; immune function |\n| Phenylalanine | 1.26g | Tyrosine \u2192 dopamine, adrenaline, thyroid hormones |\n| Methionine | 0.89g | Glutathione synthesis; SAMe for methylation |\n| Tryptophan | 0.38g | Serotonin precursor; sleep regulation |\n| Histidine | 0.96g | Carnosine synthesis; intramuscular buffering |\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] | Satiety + vitamin absorption | Avocado''s fat slows gastric emptying, extending satiety from lean protein; monounsaturated fat also carries fat-soluble vitamins and potassium supports muscle recovery |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] | Antioxidant cover | High protein throughput generates oxidative byproducts; anthocyanins counteract exercise-induced oxidative stress alongside protein-driven tissue repair |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] | Complementary fat profile | Alternating chicken (very high leucine, lean) and salmon (omega-3 rich) across the week provides complementary nutrients neither delivers alone |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Nutrient timing pair | Oats 2\u20133 hours pre-training for sustained glycogen; chicken post-training for MPS \u2014 the classic nutrient timing combination |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] How much per meal and per day?\n> **Optimal per meal: 150\u2013200g cooked** \u2014 provides 46\u201362g protein and clears the leucine threshold reliably\n>\n> **Diminishing returns above 300g per sitting:** muscle protein synthesis does not scale linearly with protein dose; the excess is oxidised for energy or converted to urea, not converted to muscle tissue. Two moderate meals are more effective than one very large serving.\n>\n> **Weekly variety:** chicken should not be the only protein source. Aim for 2\u20133 salmon servings per week for omega-3 balance \u2014 chicken provides almost none (60mg/100g vs. salmon''s 1,800mg). Eggs complement chicken by adding choline and fat-soluble vitamins that chicken lacks.\n>\n> **No cholesterol concern:** 85mg per 100g is low relative to eggs (372mg/100g); chicken breast is one of the most lipid-benign protein sources available.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nZero glycaemic impact. High protein density supports muscle protein synthesis after resistance training \u2014 muscle is the primary tissue for glucose disposal, making lean mass directly protective against insulin resistance. The niacin content makes chicken breast one of the best dietary sources of NAD+ precursors.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 150g):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 90mg of 2,000mg target\n- Antioxidants: low\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nGrilled with avocado and a green salad \u00b7 Sliced over spinach with olive oil and lemon \u00b7 Meal-prepped in bulk for the week \u00b7 Post-training with blueberries\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database classified Chicken Breast as **Processing Level: Whole/Unprocessed**, **Fermented: No** \u2014 consistent with this file. Its serving reference was 100g (assumed standard reference) = 165 cal / 31g protein / 3.6g fat / 0g carbs \u2014 matches the Per 100g figures above exactly.\n", "portion_state": "cooked", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "165", "Protein": "31.0g", "Carbs": "0g", "Sugar": "0g", "Fat": "3.6g", "Sat. Fat": "1.0g", "Fibre": "0g", "Cholesterol": "85mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount (cooked)", "Cal", "Protein", "Fat", "Cholesterol", "Leucine"], "rows": [{"Amount (cooked)": "100g", "Cal": "165", "Protein": "31.0g", "Fat": "3.6g", "Cholesterol": "85mg", "Leucine": "2.5g"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "150g", "Cal": "248", "Protein": "46.5g", "Fat": "5.4g", "Cholesterol": "128mg", "Leucine": "3.7g"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "200g", "Cal": "330", "Protein": "62.0g", "Fat": "7.2g", "Cholesterol": "170mg", "Leucine": "4.9g"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "250g", "Cal": "413", "Protein": "77.5g", "Fat": "9.0g", "Cholesterol": "213mg", "Leucine": "6.2g"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Selenium", "Per 100g": "27\u00b5g", "% RDA": "49%", "Role": "Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "210mg", "% RDA": "17%", "Role": "ATP production; bone health"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "1.0mg", "% RDA": "9\u201313%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "256mg", "% RDA": "5%", "Role": "Electrolyte balance; muscle contraction"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "29mg", "% RDA": "7%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; muscle relaxation"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "0.7mg", "% RDA": "4\u20139%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain"}, {"Mineral": "Sodium", "Per 100g": "74mg", "% RDA": "\u2014", "Role": "Fluid balance"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Niacin (B3)", "Per 100g": "13.7mg", "% RDA": "86\u201396%", "Role": "NAD+ precursor \u2014 the essential cofactor for mitochondrial energy production"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin B6", "Per 100g": "0.9mg", "% RDA": "69%", "Role": "Protein metabolism; neurotransmitter synthesis; glycogen breakdown"}, {"Vitamin": "Choline", "Per 100g": "85mg", "% RDA": "16%", "Role": "Mitochondrial membrane health; neurotransmitter function"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin B12", "Per 100g": "0.3\u00b5g", "% RDA": "13%", "Role": "Mitochondrial energy production; nerve function"}]}, {"headers": ["Amino Acid", "Per 100g", "Sports Relevance"], "rows": [{"Amino Acid": "Leucine", "Per 100g": "2.47g", "Sports Relevance": "Highest of common foods \u2014 primary mTOR trigger for MPS"}, {"Amino Acid": "Lysine", "Per 100g": "2.67g", "Sports Relevance": "Collagen synthesis; carnitine production"}, {"Amino Acid": "Isoleucine", "Per 100g": "1.43g", "Sports Relevance": "BCAA; glucose uptake into muscle during exercise"}, {"Amino Acid": "Valine", "Per 100g": "1.49g", "Sports Relevance": "BCAA; muscle glycogen sparing"}, {"Amino Acid": "Threonine", "Per 100g": "1.35g", "Sports Relevance": "Collagen and elastin; immune function"}, {"Amino 