roduction"}, {"Amino Acid": "Isoleucine", "Per 100g": "1.43g", "Sports Relevance": "BCAA; glucose uptake into muscle during exercise"}, {"Amino Acid": "Valine", "Per 100g": "1.49g", "Sports Relevance": "BCAA; muscle glycogen sparing"}, {"Amino Acid": "Threonine", "Per 100g": "1.35g", "Sports Relevance": "Collagen and elastin; immune function"}, {"Amino Acid": "Phenylalanine", "Per 100g": "1.26g", "Sports Relevance": "Tyrosine \u2192 dopamine, adrenaline, thyroid hormones"}, {"Amino Acid": "Methionine", "Per 100g": "0.89g", "Sports Relevance": "Glutathione synthesis; SAMe for methylation"}, {"Amino Acid": "Tryptophan", "Per 100g": "0.38g", "Sports Relevance": "Serotonin precursor; sleep regulation"}, {"Amino Acid": "Histidine", "Per 100g": "0.96g", "Sports Relevance": "Carnosine synthesis; intramuscular buffering"}], "food_synergies": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Satiety + vitamin absorption", "Mechanism": "Avocado''s fat slows gastric emptying, extending satiety from lean protein; monounsaturated fat also carries fat-soluble vitamins and potassium supports muscle recovery"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]]", "Synergy": "Antioxidant cover", "Mechanism": "High protein throughput generates oxidative byproducts; anthocyanins counteract exercise-induced oxidative stress alongside protein-driven tissue repair"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Complementary fat profile", "Mechanism": "Alternating chicken (very high leucine, lean) and salmon (omega-3 rich) across the week provides complementary nutrients neither delivers alone"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]", "Synergy": "Nutrient timing pair", "Mechanism": "Oats 2\u20133 hours pre-training for sustained glycogen; chicken post-training for MPS \u2014 the classic nutrient timing combination"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "protein", "calories_per_100g": "165", "protein_per_100g": "31.0", "carbs_per_100g": "0.0", "sugar_per_100g": "0.0", "added_sugar": "false", "fat_per_100g": "3.6", "saturated_fat_per_100g": "1.0", "fibre_per_100g": "0.0", "cholesterol_per_100g": "85", "omega3_per_100g_mg": "60", "iron_per_100g": "0.7", "zinc_per_100g": "1.0", "magnesium_per_100g": "29", "potassium_per_100g": "256", "sodium_per_100g": "74", "selenium_per_100g_\u00b5g": "27", "choline_per_100g_mg": "85", "vitamin_c_per_100g_mg": "0", "vitamin_d_per_100g_iu": "4", "vitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g": "0.3", "complete_protein": "true", "leucine_per_100g": "2.47", "antioxidant_level": "low", "probiotic": "false", "refined": "false", "seed_oil": "false", "body_systems": "", "serving_unit": "g", "serving_unit_g": "1", "typical_serving": "150g cooked", "typical_serving_g": "150", "glycaemic_index": "0", "mitochondrial_benefit": "high", "callouts": [{"type": "success", "title": "Sports Nutrition Snapshot", "content": "**Complete protein** \u00b7 **2.47g leucine / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 0** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\nThe highest-leucine common food and the most efficient lean protein for hitting daily targets without overshooting calories."}, {"type": "quote", "title": "\"Lean protein is the foundation of every body composition goal \u2014 it preserves muscle, drives satiety, and costs almost nothing in fat or carbohydrate.\" \u2014 Jeff Nippard", "content": ""}, {"type": "warning", "title": "Raw vs. Cooked Weight", "content": "200g raw chicken breast \u2192 ~150g cooked (roughly 25% water loss during cooking). Always weigh after cooking, or account for the shrinkage when tracking raw weight."}, {"type": "tip", "title": "Leucine at 150g", "content": "A 150g serving delivers ~3.7g leucine \u2014 well above the ~2.5\u20133g threshold required to maximally stimulate muscle protein synthesis per meal. Chicken breast is one of the most reliable single-food MPS triggers available."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Niacin (B3) and NAD+", "content": "Chicken breast is one of the richest dietary sources of niacin \u2014 the precursor to NAD+. NAD+ is the central electron carrier in mitochondrial energy production; declining NAD+ is a primary mechanism of mitochondrial ageing. A 150g serving covers ~90% of the daily niacin requirement."}, {"type": "tip", "title": "Complete Protein \u2014 Highest Common Leucine Source", "content": "Chicken breast contains all 9 essential amino acids and has the highest leucine concentration of any commonly eaten protein food \u2014 higher than beef, eggs, or salmon on a per-gram basis."}, {"type": "info", "title": "How much per meal and per day?", "content": "**Optimal per meal: 150\u2013200g cooked** \u2014 provides 46\u201362g protein and clears the leucine threshold reliably\n\n**Diminishing returns above 300g per sitting:** muscle protein synthesis does not scale linearly with protein dose; the excess is oxidised for energy or converted to urea, not converted to muscle tissue. Two moderate meals are more effective than one very large serving.\n\n**Weekly variety:** chicken should not be the only protein source. Aim for 2\u20133 salmon servings per week for omega-3 balance \u2014 chicken provides almost none (60mg/100g vs. salmon''s 1,800mg). Eggs complement chicken by adding choline and fat-soluble vitamins that chicken lacks.\n\n**No cholesterol concern:** 85mg per 100g is low relative to eggs (372mg/100g); chicken breast is one of the most lipid-benign protein sources available."}], "quotes": ["\"Lean protein is the foundation of every body composition goal \u2014 it preserves muscle, drives satiety, and costs almost nothing in fat or carbohydrate.\" \u2014 Jeff Nippard"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] How much per meal and per day?\n> **Optimal per meal: 150\u2013200g cooked** \u2014 provides 46\u201362g protein and clears the leucine threshold reliably\n>\n> **Diminishing returns above 300g per sitting:** muscle protein synthesis does not scale linearly with protein dose; the excess is oxidised for energy or converted to urea, not converted to muscle tissue. Two moderate meals are more effective than one very large serving.\n>\n> **Weekly variety:** chicken should not be the only protein source. Aim for 2\u20133 salmon servings per week for omega-3 balance \u2014 chicken provides almost none (60mg/100g vs. salmon''s 1,800mg). Eggs complement chicken by adding choline and fat-soluble vitamins that chicken lacks.\n>\n> **No cholesterol concern:** 85mg per 100g is low relative to eggs (372mg/100g); chicken breast is one of the most lipid-benign protein sources available.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "Grilled with avocado and a green salad \u00b7 Sliced over spinach with olive oil and lemon \u00b7 Meal-prepped in bulk for the week \u00b7 Post-training with blueberries\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Zero glycaemic impact. High protein density supports muscle protein synthesis after resistance training \u2014 muscle is the primary tissue for glucose disposal, making lean mass directly protective against insulin resistance. The niacin content makes chicken breast one of the best dietary sources of NAD+ precursors.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 150g):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 90mg of 2,000mg target\n- Antioxidants: low\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Chickpeas', 'carb', 110, 6, 18, 2, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "41e3831e65339d30339215167401d9fd", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/carb\nfood_category: carb\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Carb\nserving_unit: \"1/2 cup\"\ncalories_per_unit: 110\nprotein_per_unit_g: 6\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 18\nfat_per_unit_g: 2\nfiber_per_unit_g: 4\nsugar_per_unit_g: 0\nprocessing_level: \"Minimally Processed\"\nfermented: false\n---\n# Chickpeas\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries.\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Fiber |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1/2 cup | 110 | 6g | 18g | 0g | 2g | 4g |\n\n## Micronutrients (per unit)\n\n| Nutrient | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Calcium | 40mg |\n| Iron | 1.5mg |\n| Potassium | 175mg |\n| Sodium | 0mg |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Carb\n- **Processing Level:** Minimally Processed\n- **Fermented:** No\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Fiber"], "rows": [{"Unit": "1/2 cup", "Calories": "110", "Protein": "6g", "Carbs": "18g", "Sugar": "0g", "Fat": "2g", "Fiber": "4g"}]}, {"headers": ["Nutrient", "Amount"], "rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "40mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "1.5mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "175mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "0mg"}]}], "unit": "1/2 cup", "calories": "110", "protein": "6g", "carbs": "18g", "sugar": "0g", "fat": "2g", "fiber": "4g", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "40mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "1.5mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "175mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "0mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "carb", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Carb", "serving_unit": "1/2 cup", "calories_per_unit": "110", "protein_per_unit_g": "6", "carbs_per_unit_g": "18", "fat_per_unit_g": "2", "fiber_per_unit_g": "4", "sugar_per_unit_g": "0", "processing_level": "Minimally Processed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Costco Bread', 'carb', 220, 9, 42, 4, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "8c67c1565897bc46f1365d62971b6dc4", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/carb\n  - costco\nfood_category: carb\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Carb\nserving_unit: \"2 slices (90g)\"\nserving_unit_g: 90\ncalories_per_unit: 220\nprotein_per_unit_g: 9\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 42\nfat_per_unit_g: 4\nfiber_per_unit_g: 8\nsugar_per_unit_g: 7\ncholesterol_per_unit_mg: 0\nprocessing_level: \"Processed\"\nfermented: false\n---\n# Costco Bread\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries.\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Fiber | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 slices (90g) | 220 | 9g | 42g | 7g | 4g | 8g | 0mg |\n\n## Micronutrients (per unit)\n\n| Nutrient | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Calcium | 40mg |\n| Iron | 2mg |\n| Magnesium | 70mg |\n| Potassium | 200mg |\n| Sodium | 280mg |\n| Zinc | 1.5mg |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Carb\n- **Processing Level:** Processed\n- **Fermented:** No\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Fiber", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Unit": "2 slices (90g)", "Calories": "220", "Protein": "9g", "Carbs": "42g", "Sugar": "7g", "Fat": "4g", "Fiber": "8g", "Cholesterol": "0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Nutrient", "Amount"], "rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "40mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "2mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Magnesium", "Amount": "70mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "200mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "280mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Zinc", "Amount": "1.5mg"}]}], "unit": "2 slices (90g)", "calories": "220", "protein": "9g", "carbs": "42g", "sugar": "7g", "fat": "4g", "fiber": "8g", "cholesterol": "0mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "40mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "2mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Magnesium", "Amount": "70mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "200mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "280mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Zinc", "Amount": "1.5mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "carb", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Carb", "serving_unit": "2 slices (90g)", "serving_unit_g": "90", "calories_per_unit": "220", "protein_per_unit_g": "9", "carbs_per_unit_g": "42", "fat_per_unit_g": "4", "fiber_per_unit_g": "8", "sugar_per_unit_g": "7", "cholesterol_per_unit_mg": "0", "processing_level": "Processed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Cottage Cheese', 'protein', 180, 28, 14, 5, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "439d364c3557b6e6228fef299d9475cd", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/protein\n  - nutrition/food/fermented\nfood_category: protein\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Protein\nserving_unit: \"1 cup\"\ncalories_per_unit: 180\nprotein_per_unit_g: 28\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 14\nfat_per_unit_g: 5\nfiber_per_unit_g: 0\nsugar_per_unit_g: 8\ncholesterol_per_unit_mg: 30\nprocessing_level: \"Processed\"\nfermented: true\n---\n# Cottage Cheese\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries.\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Fiber | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 cup | 180 | 28g | 14g | 8g | 5g | 0g | 30mg |\n\n## Micronutrients (per unit)\n\n| Nutrient | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Calcium | 250mg |\n| Iron | 0.2mg |\n| Potassium | 200mg |\n| Sodium | 880mg |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Protein\n- **Processing Level:** Processed\n- **Fermented:** Yes\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Fiber", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Unit": "1 cup", "Calories": "180", "Protein": "28g", "Carbs": "14g", "Sugar": "8g", "Fat": "5g", "Fiber": "0g", "Cholesterol": "30mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Nutrient", "Amount"], "rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "250mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.2mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "200mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "880mg"}]}], "unit": "1 cup", "calories": "180", "protein": "28g", "carbs": "14g", "sugar": "8g", "fat": "5g", "fiber": "0g", "cholesterol": "30mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "250mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.2mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "200mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "880mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "protein", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Protein", "serving_unit": "1 cup", "calories_per_unit": "180", "protein_per_unit_g": "28", "carbs_per_unit_g": "14", "fat_per_unit_g": "5", "fiber_per_unit_g": "0", "sugar_per_unit_g": "8", "cholesterol_per_unit_mg": "30", "processing_level": "Processed", "fermented": "true", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Eggs', 'protein', 143, 12.6, 0.7, 9.5, 0.0, 180, false, '{"content_hash": "d11927e68e42d1554ab81ef379312404", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/protein\nfood_category: protein\n# Macros per 100g\ncalories_per_100g: 143\nprotein_per_100g: 12.6\ncarbs_per_100g: 0.7\nsugar_per_100g: 0.4\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 9.5\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 3.1\nfibre_per_100g: 0.0\ncholesterol_per_100g: 372\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 180\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 1.8\nzinc_per_100g: 1.3\nmagnesium_per_100g: 12\npotassium_per_100g: 138\nsodium_per_100g: 142\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 30\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 294\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 0\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 82\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 1.4\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: true\nleucine_per_100g: 1.09\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: moderate\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - brain\n  - muscles\n  - eyes\n  - mitochondria\n  - immune\n  - hormones\n  - bones\n# Serving\nserving_unit: egg\nserving_unit_g: 50\ntypical_serving: 2 eggs\ntypical_serving_g: 100\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 0\nmitochondrial_benefit: high\n---\n# Eggs\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **Complete protein** \u00b7 **1.09g leucine / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 0** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> The most complete whole food available \u2014 every essential amino acid, four fat-soluble vitamins, and the richest dietary source of choline.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"The egg is the most complete food nature produces \u2014 every amino acid, every fat-soluble vitamin, and choline for the brain.\" \u2014 Mark Hyman\n\nWhole eggs deliver complete protein alongside fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), choline for mitochondrial membrane integrity, and near-zero glucose impact. The yolk contains the majority of the nutrition \u2014 discarding it removes most of what makes eggs worth eating.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 143 | 12.6g | 0.7g | 0.4g | 9.5g | 3.1g | 0g | 372mg |\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Protein | Fat | Cholesterol | Omega-3 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 egg (50g) | 72 | 6.3g | 4.8g | 186mg | 90mg |\n| 2 eggs (100g) | 143 | 12.6g | 9.5g | 372mg | 180mg |\n| 3 eggs (150g) | 215 | 18.9g | 14.3g | 558mg | 270mg |\n| 4 eggs (200g) | 286 | 25.2g | 19.0g | 744mg | 360mg |\n\n> [!info] Carbs are negligible across all serving sizes and omitted from Quick Reference for clarity.\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Selenium | 30\u00b5g | 55% | Antioxidant enzyme cofactor; thyroid function |\n| Phosphorus | 198mg | 28% | ATP production; bone mineralisation |\n| Iron | 1.8mg | 10\u201322% | Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain |\n| Zinc | 1.3mg | 12\u201316% | Protein synthesis; immune function; testosterone |\n| Calcium | 56mg | 6% | Muscle contraction; nerve signalling |\n| Magnesium | 12mg | 3% | ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions |\n| Potassium | 138mg | 3% | Electrolyte balance; muscle function |\n| Sodium | 142mg | \u2014 | Fluid balance; nerve conduction |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Choline | 294mg | 54\u201371% | Mitochondrial membrane structure; acetylcholine synthesis |\n| Vitamin B12 | 1.4\u00b5g | 58% | Mitochondrial energy production; red blood cell formation |\n| Vitamin A | 160\u00b5g | 18\u201322% | Cell signalling; immune function; vision |\n| Vitamin D | 82 IU | 10% | Mitochondrial biogenesis; immune regulation; bone |\n\n> [!info] Choline\n> Eggs are the single richest dietary source of choline \u2014 essential for cardiolipin (the phospholipid that forms the inner mitochondrial membrane) and acetylcholine (the neurotransmitter that drives every muscle contraction).\n\n---\n\n## Amino Acid Profile\n\n> [!tip] Complete Protein\n> Eggs contain all 9 essential amino acids and are often used as the reference protein (biological value: 100) against which all other proteins are measured.\n\n| Amino Acid | Per 100g | Sports Relevance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Leucine | 1.09g | Primary mTOR activator \u2014 triggers muscle protein synthesis |\n| Lysine | 0.91g | Collagen synthesis; carnitine production for fat metabolism |\n| Phenylalanine | 0.68g | Precursor to dopamine, adrenaline, and thyroid hormones |\n| Valine | 0.77g | BCAA; muscle glycogen sparing during endurance work |\n| Threonine | 0.56g | Immune function; collagen and elastin production |\n| Isoleucine | 0.54g | BCAA; glucose uptake into muscle during exercise |\n| Histidine | 0.30g | Carnosine synthesis; intramuscular acid buffering |\n| Methionine | 0.38g | Glutathione synthesis; methylation reactions |\n| Tryptophan | 0.17g | Serotonin precursor; sleep quality regulation |\n\n> [!tip] Leucine threshold\n> ~2.5\u20133g leucine per meal is required to maximally stimulate muscle protein synthesis. **3 eggs = ~1.6g leucine** \u2014 combine with a second protein source (salmon, chicken) to reliably clear the threshold.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Glucose blunting | Egg fat and protein slows gastric emptying \u2014 significantly reduces the glucose spike from oat carbohydrates when eaten first or together |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] | Fat-soluble vitamin absorption | Avocado''s monounsaturated fat acts as a carrier for eggs'' fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) \u2014 co-ingestion substantially improves absorption |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] | Choline + DHA synergy | Dietary choline and DHA combine to form phosphatidylcholine \u2014 the primary structural phospholipid of mitochondrial and neuronal membranes; eating both maximises substrate availability |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] | Antioxidant cover for training recovery | Anthocyanins reduce exercise-induced oxidative stress while egg protein drives tissue repair \u2014 an effective post-training pairing |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!warning] Cholesterol \u2014 the nuanced picture\n> One large egg contains **~186mg dietary cholesterol** \u2014 historically flagged under the old <300mg/day guideline.\n>\n> The current picture is more nuanced:\n> - **~75% of people are hypo-responders** \u2014 dietary cholesterol has minimal effect on their LDL; the liver compensates by producing less of its own\n> - **~25% of people are hyper-responders** \u2014 dietary cholesterol does raise LDL meaningfully; if this is you, eggs warrant closer monitoring\n> - The 2020 US Dietary Guidelines removed the specific 300mg/day cap, acknowledging individual variation\n> - **Saturated fat is a bigger LDL driver than dietary cholesterol** for most people \u2014 and eggs are moderate in saturated fat (3.1g/100g)\n>\n> **Practical range for active individuals: 2\u20134 whole eggs per day** is well-supported in the literature for metabolically healthy people. Monitor LDL particle size (not just total LDL) and HDL if eating at the higher end \u2014 eggs tend to raise both, and the ratio often does not worsen.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nNear-zero glycaemic impact. The fat and protein combination maximises satiety and slows gastric emptying, blunting the glucose spike from any carbohydrates eaten at the same meal. Choline is a structural component of cardiolipin \u2014 the phospholipid central to the inner mitochondrial membrane and efficient ATP production.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 2 eggs):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 180mg of 2,000mg target\n- Antioxidants: moderate (lutein, zeaxanthin, selenium)\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nScrambled or poached in olive oil \u00b7 Hard-boiled as a portable snack \u00b7 3 eggs with 60g oats and blueberries at breakfast (~29g protein, improved leucine threshold when paired with another protein)\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chicken Breast]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database classified Eggs as **Processing Level: Whole/Unprocessed**, **Fermented: No** \u2014 consistent with this file. Its serving reference was 1 egg (50g) = 70 cal / 6g protein / 5g fat / 0g carbs / 185mg cholesterol / 1mcg vitamin D / 30mg calcium / 70mg potassium / 70mg sodium / 0.9mg iron \u2014 in line with the Quick Reference table above.\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "143", "Protein": "12.6g", "Carbs": "0.7g", "Sugar": "0.4g", "Fat": "9.5g", "Sat. Fat": "3.1g", "Fibre": "0g", "Cholesterol": "372mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Protein", "Fat", "Cholesterol", "Omega-3"], "rows": [{"Amount": "1 egg (50g)", "Cal": "72", "Protein": "6.3g", "Fat": "4.8g", "Cholesterol": "186mg", "Omega-3": "90mg"}, {"Amount": "2 eggs (100g)", "Cal": "143", "Protein": "12.6g", "Fat": "9.5g", "Cholesterol": "372mg", "Omega-3": "180mg"}, {"Amount": "3 eggs (150g)", "Cal": "215", "Protein": "18.9g", "Fat": "14.3g", "Cholesterol": "558mg", "Omega-3": "270mg"}, {"Amount": "4 eggs (200g)", "Cal": "286", "Protein": "25.2g", "Fat": "19.0g", "Cholesterol": "744mg", "Omega-3": "360mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Selenium", "Per 100g": "30\u00b5g", "% RDA": "55%", "Role": "Antioxidant enzyme cofactor; thyroid function"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "198mg", "% RDA": "28%", "Role": "ATP production; bone mineralisation"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "1.8mg", "% RDA": "10\u201322%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "1.3mg", "% RDA": "12\u201316%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; immune function; testosterone"}, {"Mineral": "Calcium", "Per 100g": "56mg", "% RDA": "6%", "Role": "Muscle contraction; nerve signalling"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "12mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "138mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "Electrolyte balance; muscle function"}, {"Mineral": "Sodium", "Per 100g": "142mg", "% RDA": "\u2014", "Role": "Fluid balance; nerve conduction"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Choline", "Per 100g": "294mg", "% RDA": "54\u201371%", "Role": "Mitochondrial membrane structure; acetylcholine synthesis"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin B12", "Per 100g": "1.4\u00b5g", "% RDA": "58%", "Role": "Mitochondrial energy production; red blood cell formation"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin A", "Per 100g": "160\u00b5g", "% RDA": "18\u201322%", "Role": "Cell signalling; immune function; vision"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin D", "Per 100g": "82 IU", "% RDA": "10%", "Role": "Mitochondrial biogenesis; immune regulation; bone"}]}, {"headers": ["Amino Acid", "Per 100g", "Sports Relevance"], "rows": [{"Amino Acid": "Leucine", "Per 100g": "1.09g", "Sports Relevance": "Primary mTOR activator \u2014 triggers muscle protein synthesis"}, {"Amino Acid": "Lysine", "Per 100g": "0.91g", "Sports Relevance": "Collagen synthesis; carnitine productio