oods/Chicken Breast]]\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Near-zero glycaemic impact. The fat and protein combination maximises satiety and slows gastric emptying, blunting the glucose spike from any carbohydrates eaten at the same meal. Choline is a structural component of cardiolipin \u2014 the phospholipid central to the inner mitochondrial membrane and efficient ATP production.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 2 eggs):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 180mg of 2,000mg target\n- Antioxidants: moderate (lutein, zeaxanthin, selenium)\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Flaxseeds (Ground)', 'fat', 90, 3, 4, 7, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "5d276c93a3e380e811c01f8d30fe00d2", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/fat\nfood_category: fat\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Fat\nserving_unit: \"2 tbsp\"\ncalories_per_unit: 90\nprotein_per_unit_g: 3\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 4\nfat_per_unit_g: 7\nsugar_per_unit_g: 0\nprocessing_level: \"Minimally Processed\"\nfermented: false\n---\n# Flaxseeds (Ground)\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries.\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 tbsp | 90 | 3g | 4g | 0g | 7g |\n\n## Micronutrients (per unit)\n\n| Nutrient | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Calcium | 50mg |\n| Iron | 0.75mg |\n| Potassium | 125mg |\n| Sodium | 0mg |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Fat\n- **Processing Level:** Minimally Processed\n- **Fermented:** No\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat"], "rows": [{"Unit": "2 tbsp", "Calories": "90", "Protein": "3g", "Carbs": "4g", "Sugar": "0g", "Fat": "7g"}]}, {"headers": ["Nutrient", "Amount"], "rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "50mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "125mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "0mg"}]}], "unit": "2 tbsp", "calories": "90", "protein": "3g", "carbs": "4g", "sugar": "0g", "fat": "7g", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "50mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "125mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "0mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "fat", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Fat", "serving_unit": "2 tbsp", "calories_per_unit": "90", "protein_per_unit_g": "3", "carbs_per_unit_g": "4", "fat_per_unit_g": "7", "sugar_per_unit_g": "0", "processing_level": "Minimally Processed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Greek Yogurt', 'fermented-protein', 97, 9.0, 3.6, 5.0, 0.0, 50, false, '{"content_hash": "a578aa7b2b5cf7152c24f450ce48c2d1", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/protein\n  - nutrition/food/fermented\nfood_category: fermented-protein\n# Macros per 100g (whole-milk, plain, unsweetened)\ncalories_per_100g: 97\nprotein_per_100g: 9.0\ncarbs_per_100g: 3.6\nsugar_per_100g: 3.2\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 5.0\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 3.2\nfibre_per_100g: 0.0\ncholesterol_per_100g: 17\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 50\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 0.1\nzinc_per_100g: 0.6\nmagnesium_per_100g: 11\npotassium_per_100g: 141\nsodium_per_100g: 36\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 9.7\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 15\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 0\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 0\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0.75\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: true\nleucine_per_100g: 0.9\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: low\nprobiotic: true\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - gut\n  - muscles\n  - bones\n  - immune\n# Serving\nserving_unit: g\nserving_unit_g: 1\ntypical_serving: 200g\ntypical_serving_g: 200\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 11\nmitochondrial_benefit: moderate\n---\n# Greek Yogurt\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **Probiotic: Yes** \u00b7 **Complete protein** \u00b7 **GI: 11** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: Moderate**\n> One of the most practical daily probiotic sources \u2014 and a concentrated protein that doubles as gut-microbiome fuel.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Fermented foods are one of the most direct ways to replenish and diversify the gut microbiome \u2014 and the microbiome is the foundation of metabolic and immune health.\" \u2014 Casey Means\n\nWhole-milk plain Greek yogurt is strained to remove whey, concentrating protein to roughly double that of regular yogurt. It delivers live bacterial cultures (Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus at minimum), complete protein, calcium, and B12 \u2014 with very low glycaemic impact. The fermentation process partially pre-digests the lactose, making it tolerable for many people who struggle with regular dairy.\n\n> [!warning] Unsweetened whole-milk only\n> Flavoured Greek yogurts typically contain 15\u201325g added sugar per 200g serving \u2014 this reverses the metabolic benefit. Low-fat versions remove the saturated fat that contributes to satiety and replaces it with more lactose. **Whole-milk, plain, unsweetened** is the only version aligned with the Good Energy framework.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g (whole-milk, plain, unsweetened)\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 97 | 9.0g | 3.6g | 3.2g | 5.0g | 3.2g | 0g | 17mg |\n\n> [!info] Sugar is naturally occurring lactose \u2014 not added sugar. Fermentation partially converts lactose, which is why Greek yogurt is lower in carbs than regular yogurt.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Protein | Fat | Carbs | Leucine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 100g | 97 | 9.0g | 5.0g | 3.6g | 0.9g |\n| 150g | 146 | 13.5g | 7.5g | 5.4g | 1.35g |\n| 200g (typical bowl) | 194 | 18.0g | 10.0g | 7.2g | 1.8g |\n| 250g | 243 | 22.5g | 12.5g | 9.0g | 2.25g |\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Calcium | 111mg | 11% | Bone mineralisation; muscle contraction; nerve signalling |\n| Phosphorus | 135mg | 11% | ATP production; bone health |\n| Potassium | 141mg | 3% | Electrolyte balance; muscle function |\n| Zinc | 0.6mg | 5\u20138% | Protein synthesis; immune function |\n| Selenium | 9.7\u00b5g | 18% | Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion |\n| Magnesium | 11mg | 3% | ATP synthesis |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vitamin B12 | 0.75\u00b5g | 31% | Mitochondrial energy production; nerve function |\n| Riboflavin (B2) | 0.23mg | 18% | Mitochondrial electron transport; FAD cofactor |\n| Vitamin B5 | 0.44mg | 9% | Coenzyme A synthesis \u2014 required for fatty acid oxidation in mitochondria |\n\n---\n\n## Probiotic Profile\n\n> [!tip] Live cultures \u2014 what''s in the yogurt\n> All yogurt by definition contains live cultures if unpasteurised after fermentation. The baseline strains are:\n>\n> | Strain | Primary Actions |\n> |---|---|\n> | *Lactobacillus bulgaricus* | Ferments lactose; produces lactic acid; transient gut coloniser |\n> | *Streptococcus thermophilus* | Ferments lactose; produces lactic acid; supports mucosal immunity |\n>\n> Some brands (Siggi''s, Fage, Chobani) add additional probiotic strains (L. acidophilus, Bifidobacterium lactis). These strains are more likely to colonise the gut long-term. Check the label for \"live and active cultures\" seal.\n>\n> **Casey Means'' target: 3+ probiotic servings per day.** Greek yogurt as a breakfast or snack makes one of these servings practical and protein-rich.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] | Probiotic + prebiotic (synbiotic) | Blueberry polyphenols act as prebiotics \u2014 selectively feeding the Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains in the yogurt; this combination (probiotic + prebiotic = synbiotic) is more effective than either alone |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chia Seeds]] | Fibre + probiotic | Chia seeds provide soluble fibre that feeds the probiotic cultures and slows the digestion of yogurt''s carbohydrates; also dramatically increases the satiety of the meal |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Prebiotic fibre amplification | Beta-glucan from oats is one of the most effective prebiotic fibres for Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium; mixing with yogurt creates a gut-optimised breakfast |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Walnuts]] | Polyphenol + probiotic | Walnut polyphenols (ellagic acid) are metabolised by gut bacteria into urolithins \u2014 compounds with anti-inflammatory and mitochondrial effects only accessible via a healthy microbiome |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] Daily probiotic servings\n> **Optimal: 150\u2013200g per serving; 1\u20132 servings per day**\n>\n> Casey Means recommends 3+ servings of fermented foods daily. Greek yogurt can cover 1\u20132 of these if eaten at breakfast and as a snack; the remainder can come from kimchi, sauerkraut, or kefir.\n>\n> **Lactose intolerance:** the straining process and fermentation reduce lactose content to ~3.2g per 100g (vs. 4.8g in regular milk). Many people with mild lactose intolerance tolerate Greek yogurt well. Severely intolerant individuals should trial a small portion first.\n>\n> **Upper limit:** no meaningful upper limit for metabolically healthy individuals. Large amounts (400g+) in one sitting add significant saturated fat and calories \u2014 spread across the day is more effective than one large dose for microbiome diversity.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nThe gut microbiome is the fourth root cause of metabolic dysfunction in the Good Energy framework (gut dysbiosis). Greek yogurt directly addresses this \u2014 the live cultures populate the gut, produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), and improve the intestinal barrier that prevents endotoxins from triggering systemic inflammation. B12 supports mitochondrial energy production, and the low GI (11) means it causes almost no glucose perturbation.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 200g):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 100mg of 2,000mg target\n- Antioxidants: low\n- Probiotics: \u2713 1 of 3 daily probiotic servings\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nPlain with blueberries and chia seeds \u00b7 Layered with oats as overnight \"yogurt oats\" \u00b7 Post-training protein source \u00b7 As a base for dressings in place of mayonnaise\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chia Seeds]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Walnuts]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database has a separate \"Greek Yogurt (Plain, Non-fat)\" entry \u2014 a genuinely different variant from the whole-milk version this file documents (Notion''s own note: \"Merged from two near-identical entries in source spreadsheet\"). Per its reference unit, **3/4 cup (175g) = 100 cal / 17g protein / 8g carbs**, i.e. per 100g: ~57 cal / 9.7g protein / 4.6g carbs / ~0g fat \u2014 near-zero fat versus this file''s whole-milk figure of 5.0g fat/100g. Processing Level: Minimally Processed, Fermented: Yes (both consistent with this file). If non-fat Greek yogurt is in rotation alongside whole-milk, note the fat/satiety trade-off called out in the warning callout above \u2014 non-fat removes the saturated fat that contributes to satiety.\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "97", "Protein": "9.0g", "Carbs": "3.6g", "Sugar": "3.2g", "Fat": "5.0g", "Sat. Fat": "3.2g", "Fibre": "0g", "Cholesterol": "17mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Protein", "Fat", "Carbs", "Leucine"], "rows": [{"Amount": "100g", "Cal": "97", "Protein": "9.0g", "Fat": "5.0g", "Carbs": "3.6g", "Leucine": "0.9g"}, {"Amount": "150g", "Cal": "146", "Protein": "13.5g", "Fat": "7.5g", "Carbs": "5.4g", "Leucine": "1.35g"}, {"Amount": "200g (typical bowl)", "Cal": "194", "Protein": "18.0g", "Fat": "10.0g", "Carbs": "7.2g", "Leucine": "1.8g"}, {"Amount": "250g", "Cal": "243", "Protein": "22.5g", "Fat": "12.5g", "Carbs": "9.0g", "Leucine": "2.25g"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Calcium", "Per 100g": "111mg", "% RDA": "11%", "Role": "Bone mineralisation; muscle contraction; nerve signalling"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "135mg", "% RDA": "11%", "Role": "ATP production; bone health"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "141mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "Electrolyte balance; muscle function"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "0.6mg", "% RDA": "5\u20138%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Selenium", "Per 100g": "9.7\u00b5g", "% RDA": "18%", "Role": "Antioxidant defence; thyroid hormone conversion"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "11mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "ATP synthesis"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Vitamin B12", "Per 100g": "0.75\u00b5g", "% RDA": "31%", "Role": "Mitochondrial energy production; nerve function"}, {"Vitamin": "Riboflavin (B2)", "Per 100g": "0.23mg", "% RDA": "18%", "Role": "Mitochondrial electron transport; FAD cofactor"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin B5", "Per 100g": "0.44mg", "% RDA": "9%", "Role": "Coenzyme A synthesis \u2014 required for fatty acid oxidation in mitochondria"}]}, {"headers": ["Paired With", "Synergy", "Mechanism"], "rows": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]]", "Synergy": "Probiotic + prebiotic (synbiotic)", "Mechanism": "Blueberry polyphenols act as prebiotics \u2014 selectively feeding the Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains in the yogurt; this combination (probiotic + prebiotic = synbiotic) is more effective than either alone"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chia Seeds]]", "Synergy": "Fibre + probiotic", "Mechanism": "Chia seeds provide soluble fibre that feeds the probiotic cultures and slo