rient": "Calcium", "Amount": "40mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.8mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "300mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "760mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "produce", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Produce", "serving_unit": "1 cup", "calories_per_unit": "40", "protein_per_unit_g": "4", "carbs_per_unit_g": "8", "fat_per_unit_g": "0", "fiber_per_unit_g": "4", "sugar_per_unit_g": "4", "cholesterol_per_unit_mg": "0", "processing_level": "Minimally Processed", "fermented": "true", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Kirkland Three Berry Blend', 'fruit', 52, 1.1, 12.0, 0.5, 3.5, 90, false, '{"content_hash": "8ba18cbe6b5235cd223f21e6870c12cc", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/fruit\n  - costco\nfood_category: fruit\nbrand: \"Kirkland Signature\"\ncostco_product: true\nproduct_name: \"Kirkland Signature Three Berry Blend\"\nberry_composition: \"blueberries, blackberries, raspberries\"\n# Macros per 100g (frozen, approximate \u2014 verify against current package label)\ncalories_per_100g: 52\nprotein_per_100g: 1.1\ncarbs_per_100g: 12.0\nsugar_per_100g: 7.5\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 0.5\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 0.0\nfibre_per_100g: 3.5\ncholesterol_per_100g: 0\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 90\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 0.5\nzinc_per_100g: 0.2\nmagnesium_per_100g: 12\npotassium_per_100g: 130\nsodium_per_100g: 1\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0.2\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 8\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 18\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 0\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: false\nleucine_per_100g: 0.06\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: high\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - brain\n  - mitochondria\n  - eyes\n  - gut\n  - heart\n  - immune\n# Serving\nserving_unit: g\nserving_unit_g: 1\ntypical_serving: 80g\ntypical_serving_g: 80\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 45\nmitochondrial_benefit: high\n---\n# Kirkland Three Berry Blend\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **Three-berry anthocyanin spectrum** \u00b7 **3.5g fibre / 100g** \u00b7 **18mg vitamin C / 100g** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> A broader polyphenol profile than any single berry \u2014 and a practical Costco staple that stays nutritious frozen year-round.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Variety in polyphenol sources is not a minor detail \u2014 different anthocyanins activate different antioxidant pathways. A mix of berries covers more biological ground than the same dose of one berry.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick\n\nThe Kirkland Three Berry Blend combines blueberries, blackberries, and raspberries in a single frozen product. Each berry contributes a distinct anthocyanin and polyphenol fingerprint \u2014 blueberries for mitochondrial PGC-1\u03b1 activation, blackberries for the highest cyanidin content of the three, and raspberries for ellagic acid and additional vitamin C. The combination delivers a broader antioxidant spectrum than any single berry at the same serving size.\n\n> [!info] Frozen = nutritionally equivalent or superior\n> Frozen at peak ripeness, this blend typically retains more anthocyanins and vitamin C than out-of-season fresh berries. The frozen format also makes it a consistent year-round source regardless of seasonal availability.\n\n> [!warning] Verify current label\n> Exact macros vary slightly between production batches and packaging runs. The values here reflect the typical nutritional profile \u2014 always cross-check against the current Costco package label, particularly for fibre and sugar.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g (frozen)\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 52 | 1.1g | 12.0g | 7.5g | 0.5g | 0g | 3.5g | 0mg |\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Carbs | Sugar | Fibre | Vitamin C |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 80g | 42 | 9.6g | 6.0g | 2.8g | 14.4mg |\n| 120g | 62 | 14.4g | 9.0g | 4.2g | 21.6mg |\n| 150g | 78 | 18.0g | 11.3g | 5.3g | 27.0mg |\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Potassium | 130mg | 3% | Electrolyte balance; muscle function |\n| Magnesium | 12mg | 3% | ATP synthesis |\n| Iron | 0.5mg | 3\u20136% | Oxygen transport; enhanced with vitamin C co-ingestion |\n| Manganese | 0.4mg | 17\u201320% | Mitochondrial SOD2 antioxidant enzyme cofactor |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vitamin C | 18mg | 20% | Higher than pure blueberries (9.7mg) \u2014 antioxidant; collagen synthesis; iron absorption |\n| Vitamin K | 14\u00b5g | 12\u201316% | Bone mineralisation; coagulation |\n| Folate | 18\u00b5g | 5% | DNA synthesis; methylation |\n\n---\n\n## Polyphenol Profile \u2014 Three-Berry Spectrum\n\n> [!tip] Why the blend outperforms a single berry\n> Each berry contributes distinct polyphenol classes that activate different biological pathways. Eating the three together provides broader coverage than a larger serving of any one berry.\n\n| Berry | Primary Polyphenols | Key Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Blueberries | Delphinidin, cyanidin, petunidin, peonidin, malvidin glucosides | PGC-1\u03b1 activation \u2192 mitochondrial biogenesis; crosses blood-brain barrier |\n| Blackberries | Cyanidin-3-glucoside (highest of the three) | Anti-inflammatory; neuroprotective; cardiovascular protection |\n| Raspberries | Ellagic acid, raspberry ketones, quercetin | Ellagic acid \u2192 urolithins (gut bacteria convert to mitochondrial activators); anti-inflammatory |\n\n**Total anthocyanin content:** estimated ~200\u2013250mg per 100g \u2014 higher than cultivated blueberries alone (~165mg/100g) due to the blackberry contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Compared to Pure Blueberries\n\n| Attribute | [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] (100g) | Three Berry Blend (100g) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Calories | 57 | 52 |\n| Fibre | 2.4g | 3.5g |\n| Vitamin C | 9.7mg | 18mg |\n| Anthocyanins | ~165mg | ~200\u2013250mg (estimated) |\n| GI | 53 | 45 (lower \u2014 blackberries and raspberries have lower GI) |\n| Polyphenol diversity | Moderate | High (three distinct profiles) |\n\nThe blend wins on every metric that matters for the Good Energy framework. Where pure blueberries have an edge: wild blueberries specifically (2\u20133\u00d7 anthocyanin density), which the blend does not use.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] | Synbiotic + polyphenol diversity | Probiotic bacteria from yogurt convert raspberry ellagic acid into urolithins; the three-berry polyphenol mix selectively feeds more diverse microbial populations than a single berry |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Double anti-glycaemic | Beta-glucan + combined berry anthocyanins; lower GI than oats + blueberries alone |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] | Fat-soluble phytonutrient absorption | Avocado fat enhances absorption of fat-soluble carotenoids and polyphenols from all three berries |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chia Seeds]] | Fibre amplification + antioxidant cover | Combined fibre exceeds 7g per serving \u2014 significant prebiotic dose; chia ALA + berry polyphenols are complementary anti-inflammatory inputs |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] Same practical range as pure blueberries\n> **Optimal: 80\u2013150g per day**\n> - 80g delivers ~2.8g fibre, ~14mg vitamin C, and a meaningful polyphenol dose\n> - 120\u2013150g is appropriate when the blend is the primary fruit source for the day\n>\n> **Frozen from frozen:** no need to thaw before eating \u2014 frozen berries can be added directly to oats, yogurt, or a smoothie. Thawed berries soften quickly; use promptly after thawing.\n>\n> **No upper limit concern** for metabolically healthy individuals. Natural sugars (7.5g/100g) are well-buffered by fibre and the anti-glycaemic effects of the polyphenols.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nThe three-berry blend covers more polyphenol pathways simultaneously than any single berry \u2014 addressing the antioxidant attribute of the Good Energy framework more broadly. The ellagic acid from raspberries adds a urolithin production pathway (via gut bacteria) that pure blueberries do not provide. As a Costco staple kept frozen, it is one of the most practical high-antioxidant foods to have consistently on hand.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 80g):**\n- Fibre: 2.8g of 50g target (6%)\n- Omega-3: 72mg of 2,000mg target (trace ALA)\n- Antioxidants: high (multi-spectrum anthocyanins, ellagic acid, quercetin)\n- Probiotics: none (but polyphenols are prebiotics for diverse microbial populations)\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nStraight from frozen into Greek yogurt \u00b7 Added to oats \u00b7 Blended into a smoothie \u00b7 Thawed as a topping with chia seeds\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chia Seeds]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]]\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "52", "Protein": "1.1g", "Carbs": "12.0g", "Sugar": "7.5g", "Fat": "0.5g", "Sat. Fat": "0g", "Fibre": "3.5g", "Cholesterol": "0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fibre", "Vitamin C"], "rows": [{"Amount": "80g", "Cal": "42", "Carbs": "9.6g", "Sugar": "6.0g", "Fibre": "2.8g", "Vitamin C": "14.4mg"}, {"Amount": "120g", "Cal": "62", "Carbs": "14.4g", "Sugar": "9.0g", "Fibre": "4.2g", "Vitamin C": "21.6mg"}, {"Amount": "150g", "Cal": "78", "Carbs": "18.0g", "Sugar": "11.3g", "Fibre": "5.3g", "Vitamin C": "27.0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "130mg", "% RDA": "3%", "Role": "Electrolyte balance; muscle functi