ct dry weight throughout."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Beta-glucan threshold", "content": "3g of beta-glucan per meal is the clinically established dose for meaningful LDL reduction and glucose blunting. **60g dry oats provides ~3.4g** \u2014 just above the threshold. Going below 50g dry risks falling short of the functional dose."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Manganese and Mitochondria", "content": "Oats are one of the richest dietary sources of manganese \u2014 the essential cofactor for SOD2, the enzyme that neutralises superoxide radicals inside the mitochondrion. This is the cell''s primary internal antioxidant defence; manganese deficiency directly impairs mitochondrial function."}, {"type": "warning", "title": "Timing matters more than amount", "content": "**Optimal serving: 60\u201380g dry**\n- 60g provides the beta-glucan threshold (~3.4g) and 40.6g carbs \u2014 sufficient pre-training fuel\n- 80g provides more fibre (8.5g) but 54g carbs \u2014 more appropriate for heavy training days\n\n**Morning is strongly preferred:** glucose tolerance is highest in the morning; the same 60g oat portion causes a ~30% larger glucose spike when eaten in the evening vs. 7\u20138am. This is a circadian effect, not a food quality issue.\n\n**Not for low-carb or rest days** if carbohydrate is being reduced \u2014 60g dry = 40.6g carbs is a significant glucose load. On rest days, substitute a lower-carb option or reduce portion to 40g.\n\n**Avoid instant oats:** heat processing degrades beta-glucan structure, reducing the functional fibre dose. Rolled oats (5-minute) or steel-cut oats preserve the beta-glucan intact."}], "quotes": ["\"Oats are one of the few grains with a meaningful beta-glucan content \u2014 a fibre that has been shown in clinical trials to blunt the post-meal glucose spike and improve insulin sensitivity.\" \u2014 Casey Means"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!warning] Timing matters more than amount\n> **Optimal serving: 60\u201380g dry**\n> - 60g provides the beta-glucan threshold (~3.4g) and 40.6g carbs \u2014 sufficient pre-training fuel\n> - 80g provides more fibre (8.5g) but 54g carbs \u2014 more appropriate for heavy training days\n>\n> **Morning is strongly preferred:** glucose tolerance is highest in the morning; the same 60g oat portion causes a ~30% larger glucose spike when eaten in the evening vs. 7\u20138am. This is a circadian effect, not a food quality issue.\n>\n> **Not for low-carb or rest days** if carbohydrate is being reduced \u2014 60g dry = 40.6g carbs is a significant glucose load. On rest days, substitute a lower-carb option or reduce portion to 40g.\n>\n> **Avoid instant oats:** heat processing degrades beta-glucan structure, reducing the functional fibre dose. Rolled oats (5-minute) or steel-cut oats preserve the beta-glucan intact.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "Cooked with eggs and blueberries (breakfast) \u00b7 Overnight oats with blueberries \u00b7 Pre-workout carbohydrate source 2\u20133 hours before training\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]]", "good_energy_relevance": "Oats contribute meaningfully to the Good Energy framework''s 50g/day fibre target \u2014 one of the highest among common foods. The beta-glucan feeds the gut microbiome that produces SCFAs, which independently improve insulin sensitivity and reduce gut inflammation. Moderate GI (55) combined with beta-glucan gel means the glucose response in practice is lower than the GI number implies \u2014 especially when combined with eggs.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 60g dry):**\n- Fibre: 6.4g of 50g target (13%)\n- Omega-3: 66mg of 2,000mg target (ALA, poorly converted)\n- Antioxidants: moderate (avenanthramides, manganese-SOD2)\n- Probiotics: none (but beta-glucan is a prebiotic that feeds probiotic bacteria)\n\n--