g"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "1.75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "300mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "0mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "carb", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Carb", "serving_unit": "1/4 cup raw (45g)", "serving_unit_g": "45", "calories_per_unit": "160", "protein_per_unit_g": "5", "carbs_per_unit_g": "31", "fat_per_unit_g": "2.5", "fiber_per_unit_g": "3", "sugar_per_unit_g": "1", "processing_level": "Minimally Processed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Salmon', 'Protein', 206, 20.4, 0, 13.4, 0, 1800, false, '{"content_hash": "8c8866519baa883573c5a3b601d106f4", "full_markdown_content": "# Salmon\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **Complete protein** \u00b7 **1,800mg omega-3 / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 0** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> The highest-priority food for mitochondrial membrane integrity \u2014 EPA and DHA are literally built into every mitochondrion in the body.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Wild salmon is the most mitochondrially supportive food available \u2014 omega-3s are literally built into the membranes of every mitochondrion in your body.\" \u2014 Casey Means\n\nAtlantic salmon delivers complete protein alongside a concentrated dose of EPA and DHA \u2014 the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids that form the structural basis of mitochondrial membranes, reduce systemic inflammation, and support brain and cardiovascular health. It is also one of the few reliable dietary sources of vitamin D and astaxanthin, the most potent dietary antioxidant known.\n\n---\n\n## Nutritional Profile (per 100g cooked)\n\n| Attribute | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Food Category | Protein |\n| Calories (per 100g) | 206 |\n| Protein (per 100g) | 20.4g |\n| Carbs (per 100g) | 0g |\n| Fat (per 100g) | 13.4g |\n| Saturated Fat (per 100g) | 3.1g |\n| Fibre (per 100g) | 0g |\n| Cholesterol | 63mg |\n| Omega-3 (EPA+DHA) | 1,800mg |\n| Glycaemic Index | 0 |\n| Seed Oil | No |\n| Vitamin D | 526 IU |\n| Vitamin B12 | 2.8\u00b5g |\n| Selenium | 30\u00b5g |\n| Choline | 94mg |\n| Potassium | 363mg |\n| Magnesium | 29mg |\n| Iron | 0.8mg |\n| Zinc | 0.6mg |\n| Complete Protein | Yes |\n| Leucine (per 100g) | 1.61g |\n| Antioxidant Level | High (Astaxanthin) |\n| Mitochondrial Benefit | High |\n| Target Body Systems | Brain, Heart, Muscles, Eyes, Mitochondria, Immune, Skin |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Serving Reference\n\n| Amount (cooked) | Cal | Protein | Fat | Omega-3 (EPA+DHA) | Vitamin D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 100g | 206 | 20.4g | 13.4g | 1,800mg | 526 IU |\n| 150g | 309 | 30.6g | 20.1g | 2,700mg | 789 IU |\n| 200g | 412 | 40.8g | 26.8g | 3,600mg | 1,052 IU |\n\n> [!tip] Omega-3 target at 150g\n> A single 150g serving delivers 2,700mg EPA+DHA \u2014 exceeding Casey Means'' recommended minimum of 2,000mg/day. Two servings per week covers the weekly omega-3 requirement for most people.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[Avocado]] | Anti-inflammatory fat synergy | Monounsaturated fat (avocado) + EPA/DHA (salmon) together cover the full anti-inflammatory lipid spectrum; zero glucose impact meal |\n| [[Eggs]] | Choline + DHA synergy | Choline (eggs) and DHA (salmon) are co-substrates for phosphatidylcholine synthesis |\n| [[Blueberries]] | Dual antioxidant protection | Astaxanthin (salmon) + anthocyanins (blueberries) act through different antioxidant pathways simultaneously |\n| Lemon / vitamin C | Iron absorption | Vitamin C enhances non-heme iron absorption from salmon |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] Weekly target and practical considerations\n> **Target: 2\u20133 servings per week (150\u2013200g each)**\n> - 2 servings/week = ~5,400mg EPA+DHA \u2014 covers the 2,000mg/day average across the week\n> - 3 servings/week provides additional vitamin D and astaxanthin benefits\n>\n> **Mercury:** Atlantic salmon has naturally low mercury compared to larger fish (tuna, swordfish).\n> **Wild vs. farmed:** Wild-caught salmon has higher omega-3 content (~2,500mg/100g vs. ~1,800mg farmed) and significantly more astaxanthin.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nZero glycemic impact. The EPA and DHA in salmon directly integrate into mitochondrial membranes, improving the efficiency and structural integrity of the electron transport chain. Omega-3s also reduce chronic inflammation in the Good Energy framework.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 150g):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 2,700mg of 2,000mg target \u2713 (covers daily target in one serving)\n- Antioxidants: high (astaxanthin, selenium, vitamin D)\n- Probiotics: none\n", "portion_state": "cooked", "tables": [{"headers": ["Attribute", "Value"], "rows": [{"Attribute": "Food Category", "Value": "Protein"}, {"Attribute": "Calories (per 100g)", "Value": "206"}, {"Attribute": "Protein (per 100g)", "Value": "20.4g"}, {"Attribute": "Carbs (per 100g)", "Value": "0g"}, {"Attribute": "Fat (per 100g)", "Value": "13.4g"}, {"Attribute": "Saturated Fat (per 100g)", "Value": "3.1g"}, {"Attribute": "Fibre (per 100g)", "Value": "0g"}, {"Attribute": "Cholesterol", "Value": "63mg"}, {"Attribute": "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)", "Value": "1,800mg"}, {"Attribute": "Glycaemic Index", "Value": "0"}, {"Attribute": "Seed Oil", "Value": "No"}, {"Attribute": "Vitamin D", "Value": "526 IU"}, {"Attribute": "Vitamin B12", "Value": "2.8\u00b5g"}, {"Attribute": "Selenium", "Value": "30\u00b5g"}, {"Attribute": "Choline", "Value": "94mg"}, {"Attribute": "Potassium", "Value": "363mg"}, {"Attribute": "Magnesium", "Value": "29mg"}, {"Attribute": "Iron", "Value": "0.8mg"}, {"Attribute": "Zinc", "Value": "0.6mg"}, {"Attribute": "Complete Protein", "Value": "Yes"}, {"Attribute": "Leucine (per 100g)", "Value": "1.61g"}, {"Attribute": "Antioxidant Level", "Value": "High (Astaxanthin)"}, {"Attribute": "Mitochondrial Benefit", "Value": "High"}, {"Attribute": "Target Body Systems", "Value": "Brain, Heart, Muscles, Eyes, Mitochondria, Immune, Skin"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount (cooked)", "Cal", "Protein", "Fat", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)", "Vitamin D"], "rows": [{"Amount (cooked)": "100g", "Cal": "206", "Protein": "20.4g", "Fat": "13.4g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "1,800mg", "Vitamin D": "526 IU"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "150g", "Cal": "309", "Protein": "30.6g", "Fat": "20.1g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "2,700mg", "Vitamin D": "789 IU"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "200g", "Cal": "412", "Protein": "40.8g", "Fat": "26.8g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "3,600mg", "Vitamin D": "1,052 IU"}]}, {"headers": ["Paired With", "Synergy", "Mechanism"], "rows": [{"Paired With": "[[Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Anti-inflammatory fat synergy", "Mechanism": "Monounsaturated fat (avocado) + EPA/DHA (salmon) together cover the full anti-inflammatory lipid spectrum; zero glucose impact meal"}, {"Paired With": "[[Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Choline + DHA synergy", "Mechanism": "Choline (eggs) and DHA (salmon) are co-substrates for phosphatidylcholine synthesis"}, {"Paired With": "[[Blueberries]]", "Synergy": "Dual antioxidant protection", "Mechanism": "Astaxanthin (salmon) + anthocyanins (blueberries) act through different antioxidant pathways simultaneously"}, {"Paired With": "Lemon / vitamin C", "Synergy": "Iron absorption", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C enhances non-heme iron absorption from salmon"}]}], "food_category": "Protein", "calories_per_100g": "206", "protein_per_100g": "20.4g", "carbs_per_100g": "0g", "fat_per_100g": "13.4g", "saturated_fat_per_100g": "3.1g", "fibre_per_100g": "0g", "cholesterol": "63mg", "omega-3_epadha": "1,800mg", "glycaemic_index": "0", "seed_oil": "No", "vitamin_d": "526 IU", "vitamin_b12": "2.8\u00b5g", "selenium": "30\u00b5g", "choline": "94mg", "potassium": "363mg", "magnesium": "29mg", "iron": "0.8mg", "zinc": "0.6mg", "complete_protein": "Yes", "leucine_per_100g": "1.61g", "antioxidant_level": "High (Astaxanthin)", "mitochondrial_benefit": "High", "target_body_systems": "Brain, Heart, Muscles, Eyes, Mitochondria, Immune, Skin", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Amount (cooked)": "100g", "Cal": "206", "Protein": "20.4g", "Fat": "13.4g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "1,800mg", "Vitamin D": "526 IU"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "150g", "Cal": "309", "Protein": "30.6g", "Fat": "20.1g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "2,700mg", "Vitamin D": "789 IU"}, {"Amount (cooked)": "200g", "Cal": "412", "Protein": "40.8g", "Fat": "26.8g", "Omega-3 (EPA+DHA)": "3,600mg", "Vitamin D": "1,052 IU"}], "food_synergies": [{"Paired With": "[[Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Anti-inflammatory fat synergy", "Mechanism": "Monounsaturated fat (avocado) + EPA/DHA (salmon) together cover the full anti-inflammatory lipid spectrum; zero glucose impact meal"}, {"Paired With": "[[Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Choline + DHA synergy", "Mechanism": "Choline (eggs) and DHA (salmon) are co-substrates for phosphatidylcholine synthesis"}, {"Paired With": "[[Blueberries]]", "Synergy": "Dual antioxidant protection", "Mechanism": "Astaxanthin (salmon) + anthocyanins (blueberries) act through different antioxidant pathways simultaneously"}, {"Paired With": "Lemon / vitamin C", "Synergy": "Iron absorption", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C enhances non-heme iron absorption from salmon"}], "callouts": [{"type": "success", "title": "Sports Nutrition Snapshot", "content": "**Complete protein** \u00b7 **1,800mg omega-3 / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 0** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\nThe highest-priority food for mitochondrial membrane integrity \u2014 EPA and DHA are literally built into every mitochondrion in the body."}, {"type": "quote", "title": "\"Wild salmon is the most mitochondrially supportive food available \u2014 omega-3s are literally built into the membranes of every mitochondrion in your body.\" \u2014 Casey Means", "content": ""}, {"type": "tip", "title": "Omega-3 target at 150g", "content": "A single 150g serving delivers 2,700mg EPA+DHA \u2014 exceeding Casey Means'' recommended minimum of 2,000mg/day. Two servings per week covers the weekly omega-3 requirement for most people."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Weekly target and practical considerations", "content": "**Target: 2\u20133 servings per week (150\u2013200g each)**\n- 2 servings/week = ~5,400mg EPA+DHA \u2014 covers the 2,000mg/day average across the week\n- 3 servings/week provides additional vitamin D and astaxanthin benefits\n\n**Mercury:** Atlantic salmon has naturally low mercury compared to larger fish (tuna, swordfish).\n**Wild vs. farmed:** Wild-caught salmon has higher omega-3 content (~2,500mg/100g vs. ~1,800mg farmed) and significantly more astaxanthin."}], "quotes": ["\"Wild salmon is the most mitochondrially supportive food available \u2014 omega-3s are literally built into the membranes of every mitochondrion in your body.\" \u2014 Casey Means"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] Weekly target and practical considerations\n> **Target: 2\u20133 servings per week (150\u2013200g each)**\n> - 2 servings/week = ~5,400mg EPA+DHA \u2014 covers the 2,000mg/day average across the week\n> - 3 servings/week provides additional vitamin D and astaxanthin benefits\n>\n> **Mercury:** Atlantic salmon has naturally low mercury compared to larger fish (tuna, swordfish).\n> **Wild vs. farmed:** Wild-caught salmon has higher omega-3 content (~2,500mg/100g vs. ~1,800mg farmed) and significantly more astaxanthin.\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Zero glycemic impact. The EPA and DHA in salmon directly integrate into mitochondrial membranes, improving the efficiency and structural integrity of the electron transport chain. Omega-3s also reduce chronic inflammation in the Good Energy framework.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 150g):**\n- Fibre: 0g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 2,700mg of 2,000mg target \u2713 (covers daily target in one serving)\n- Antioxidants: high (astaxanthin, selenium, vitamin D)\n- Probiotics: none"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Sauerkraut (Wildbrine)', 'produce', 10, 0.4, 2, 0, 0, 0, false, '{"content_hash": "a9c13d26c9c318671c279a6870427f85", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/produce\n  - nutrition/food/fermented\nfood_category: produce\nsource: notion\nnotion_category: Produce\nserving_unit: \"2 tbsp\"\ncalories_per_unit: 10\nprotein_per_unit_g: 0.4\ncarbs_per_unit_g: 2\nfat_per_unit_g: 0\nfiber_per_unit_g: 1\nsugar_per_unit_g: 1\nprocessing_level: \"Minimally Processed\"\nfermented: true\n---\n# Sauerkraut (Wildbrine)\n\nMigrated from Notion''s Foods database. Data is per serving **unit**, not per 100g \u2014 a sparser reference than the vault''s primary food-library entries.\n\n## Macros (per unit)\n\n| Unit | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Fiber |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 tbsp | 10 | 0.4g | 2g | 1g | 0g | 1g |\n\n## Micronutrients (per unit)\n\n| Nutrient | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Calcium | 10mg |\n| Iron | 0.1mg |\n| Potassium | 50mg |\n| Sodium | 260mg |\n\n## Classification\n\n- **Category:** Produce\n- **Processing Level:** Minimally Processed\n- **Fermented:** Yes\n", "portion_state": "unspecified", "tables": [{"headers": ["Unit", "Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Fiber"], "rows": [{"Unit": "2 tbsp", "Calories": "10", "Protein": "0.4g", "Carbs": "2g", "Sugar": "1g", "Fat": "0g", "Fiber": "1g"}]}, {"headers": ["Nutrient", "Amount"], "rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "10mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.1mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "50mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "260mg"}]}], "unit": "2 tbsp", "calories": "10", "protein": "0.4g", "carbs": "2g", "sugar": "1g", "fat": "0g", "fiber": "1g", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "10mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "0.1mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "50mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "260mg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "produce", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Produce", "serving_unit": "2 tbsp", "calories_per_unit": "10", "protein_per_unit_g": "0.4", "carbs_per_unit_g": "2", "fat_per_unit_g": "0", "fiber_per_unit_g": "1", "sugar_per_unit_g": "1", "processing_level": "Minimally Processed", "fermented": "true", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Spinach', 'vegetable', 23, 2.9, 3.6, 0.4, 2.2, 138, false, '{"content_hash": "5ad815f5a44ddb43e59e564c749f35a2", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/vegetable\nfood_category: vegetable\n# Macros per 100g (raw)\ncalories_per_100g: 23\nprotein_per_100g: 2.9\ncarbs_per_100g: 3.6\nsugar_per_100g: 0.4\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 0.4\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 0.1\nfibre_per_100g: 2.2\ncholesterol_per_100g: 0\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 138\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 2.7\nzinc_per_100g: 0.5\nmagnesium_per_100g: 79\npotassium_per_100g: 558\nsodium_per_100g: 79\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 1.0\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 19\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 28.1\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 0\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: false\nleucine_per_100g: 0.22\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: high\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - eyes\n  - mitochondria\n  - brain\n  - heart\n  - immune\n  - bones\n# Serving\nserving_unit: g\nserving_unit_g: 1\ntypical_serving: 60g raw\ntypical_serving_g: 60\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 15\nmitochondrial_benefit: high\n---\n# Spinach\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **12,198\u00b5g lutein+zeaxanthin / 100g** \u00b7 **79mg magnesium / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 15** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\n> The most micronutrient-dense leafy green \u2014 and one of the richest plant sources of magnesium, the mineral most critical for ATP production.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Dark leafy greens are the most nutrient-dense category of food on the planet \u2014 more vitamins, minerals, and phytonutrients per calorie than any other food group.\" \u2014 Casey Means\n\nSpinach is the foundational leafy green for metabolic and mitochondrial health. At 23 calories per 100g, it delivers extraordinary concentrations of magnesium (for ATP synthesis), folate (for methylation and DNA repair), vitamin K (for bone and cardiovascular health), and lutein+zeaxanthin (for eye and mitochondrial protection) \u2014 alongside CoQ10, one of the few plant foods to contain this direct mitochondrial cofactor.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g (raw)\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 23 | 2.9g | 3.6g | 0.4g | 0.4g | 0.1g | 2.2g | 0mg |\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Magnesium | Folate | Vitamin K | Lutein+Zeaxanthin |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 30g (side handful) | 7 | 24mg | 58\u00b5g | 145\u00b5g | 3,659\u00b5g |\n| 60g (large handful) | 14 | 47mg | 116\u00b5g | 290\u00b5g | 7,319\u00b5g |\n| 100g | 23 | 79mg | 194\u00b5g | 483\u00b5g | 12,198\u00b5g |\n| 150g (cooked portion) | 35 | 119mg | 291\u00b5g | 725\u00b5g | 18,297\u00b5g |\n\n> [!info] Volume loss when cooking\n> 100g raw spinach \u2192 ~15g cooked (roughly 7:1 compression). A cooked spinach portion that looks small represents a large raw volume. Cooking reduces oxalates by ~30\u201350%, improving mineral absorption.\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Potassium | 558mg | 12% | Blood pressure regulation; muscle contraction; highest of common vegetables |\n| Magnesium | 79mg | 19% | ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions; most people are deficient \u2014 spinach is one of the best dietary sources |\n| Iron | 2.7mg | 15\u201333% | Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain \u2014 non-heme iron; absorption improved substantially with vitamin C |\n| Calcium | 99mg | 10% | Bone; muscle contraction \u2014 partially blocked by oxalates; cooking improves absorption |\n| Phosphorus | 49mg | 4% | ATP production |\n| Zinc | 0.5mg | 5\u20136% | Protein synthesis; immune function |\n| Sodium | 79mg | \u2014 | Naturally occurring; not added |\n\n> [!warning] Oxalates and iron absorption\n> Spinach contains oxalic acid, which binds to iron and calcium and reduces their absorption from spinach itself. This is not a reason to avoid spinach \u2014 the iron dose is still meaningful \u2014 but pairing spinach with **vitamin C** (lemon juice, blueberries) significantly improves non-heme iron absorption. Cooking reduces oxalate content by 30\u201350%.\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vitamin K | 483\u00b5g | 402% | Bone mineralisation; arterial calcium regulation; coagulation |\n| Folate | 194\u00b5g | 49% | DNA synthesis; methylation reactions; neural development |\n| Vitamin C | 28.1mg | 31% | Antioxidant; collagen synthesis; iron absorption enhancement |\n| Vitamin A (as beta-carotene) | 469\u00b5g | 52\u201367% | Cell signalling; immune function; vision |\n| Riboflavin (B2) | 0.19mg | 15% | Mitochondrial electron transport (FAD cofactor) |\n| Vitamin E | 2.0mg | 13% | Fat-soluble antioxidant; mitochondrial membrane protection |\n\n---\n\n## Phytonutrient Profile\n\n| Compound | Per 100g | Key Actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lutein + Zeaxanthin | 12,198\u00b5g | Concentrates in the macula; protects retinal cells from oxidative damage; highest concentration of any common food |\n| Beta-carotene | 5,626\u00b5g | Provitamin A; antioxidant; anti-inflammatory |\n| Coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10) | trace\u20130.9mg | Direct mitochondrial electron transport cofactor; rare in plant foods; regenerates vitamin E |\n| Quercetin | ~13mg | Anti-inflammatory; antiviral; mast cell stabiliser |\n| Kaempferol | ~8mg | Anti-inflammatory; antiproliferative; heart protective |\n| Chlorophyll | high | Antioxidant; may bind dietary mutagens; liver protective |\n\n> [!info] CoQ10 in spinach\n> CoQ10 (ubiquinone) is a direct participant in the mitochondrial electron transport chain \u2014 it carries electrons between Complexes I/II and Complex III. Most dietary CoQ10 comes from organ meats and fatty fish; spinach is one of the few plant sources with measurable CoQ10 content.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] | Iron absorption + omega-3 | Vitamin C in spinach enhances non-heme iron absorption; omega-3 from salmon reduces the inflammation that impairs iron utilisation |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] | Fat-soluble nutrient absorption | Egg fat acts as a carrier for spinach''s fat-soluble compounds (lutein, zeaxanthin, beta-carotene, vitamin K, vitamin E) \u2014 absorption increases substantially when fat is co-ingested |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] | Fat-soluble phytonutrient absorption | Same mechanism as eggs \u2014 avocado fat is an excellent carrier for spinach carotenoids; this is one of the most evidence-backed food pairings in the carotenoid bioavailability literature |\n| Lemon juice | Iron absorption | Vitamin C from lemon directly chelates non-heme iron, converting it to the ferrous form that absorbs 2\u20133\u00d7 more efficiently |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] How much and in what form?\n> **Optimal: 60\u2013150g raw daily** (or equivalent cooked \u2014 roughly 10\u201320g cooked)\n>\n> Spinach is effectively a free food from a calorie standpoint (23 cal/100g). There is no practical upper limit for healthy individuals without kidney issues.\n>\n> **Kidney stones / oxalates:** people with a history of calcium-oxalate kidney stones should moderate raw spinach intake. Cooking significantly reduces oxalate content \u2014 cooked spinach is preferable in this case.\n>\n> **Vitamin K and anticoagulants (warfarin):** very high vitamin K intake (483\u00b5g/100g) can interfere with warfarin dosing. Consistent intake (same amount daily) is preferable to large fluctuations.\n>\n> **Raw vs. cooked:** raw spinach preserves vitamin C and folate (heat-sensitive); cooked spinach reduces oxalates and makes lutein, zeaxanthin, and beta-carotene more bioavailable (heat breaks down cell walls). Both are valuable \u2014 variety is ideal.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nSpinach is one of the most micronutrient-dense foods available \u2014 the core of Means'' first trackable attribute. The magnesium content directly supports ATP production (the mitochondrial function target); the folate supports DNA methylation; the lutein and zeaxanthin protect mitochondrial membranes from oxidative damage. As a near-zero-calorie food with negligible glucose impact, it can be added to almost any meal without disrupting macronutrient targets.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 60g raw):**\n- Fibre: 1.3g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 83mg of 2,000mg target (ALA, minimal)\n- Antioxidants: high (lutein/zeaxanthin, beta-carotene, quercetin, CoQ10)\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nRaw in a salad with salmon, avocado, and lemon \u00b7 Wilted in olive oil as a side \u00b7 Blended into a smoothie (flavour is masked by fruit) \u00b7 Base layer under eggs\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chicken Breast]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database classified Spinach as **Processing Level: Whole/Unprocessed**, **Fermented: No** \u2014 consistent with this file. Its serving reference was 1 cup raw (assumed) = 7 cal / 0.9g protein / 1.1g carbs / 0.1g fat / 0.1g sugar \u2014 no new facts beyond what''s already covered above.\n", "portion_state": "cooked", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "23", "Protein": "2.9g", "Carbs": "3.6g", "Sugar": "0.4g", "Fat": "0.4g", "Sat. Fat": "0.1g", "Fibre": "2.2g", "Cholesterol": "0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Magnesium", "Folate", "Vitamin K", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin"], "rows": [{"Amount": "30g (side handful)", "Cal": "7", "Magnesium": "24mg", "Folate": "58\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "145\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "3,659\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "60g (large handful)", "Cal": "14", "Magnesium": "47mg", "Folate": "116\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "290\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "7,319\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "100g", "Cal": "23", "Magnesium": "79mg", "Folate": "194\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "483\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "12,198\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "150g (cooked portion)", "Cal": "35", "Magnesium": "119mg", "Folate": "291\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "725\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "18,297\u00b5g"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "558mg", "% RDA": "12%", "Role": "Blood pressure regulation; muscle contraction; highest of common vegetables"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "79mg", "% RDA": "19%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions; most people are deficient \u2014 spinach is one of the best dietary sources"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "2.7mg", "% RDA": "15\u201333%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain \u2014 non-heme iron; absorption improved substantially with vitamin C"}, {"Mineral": "Calcium", "Per 100g": "99mg", "% RDA": "10%", "Role": "Bone; muscle contraction \u2014 partially blocked by oxalates; cooking improves absorption"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "49mg", "% RDA": "4%", "Role": "ATP production"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "0.5mg", "% RDA": "5\u20136%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Sodium", "Per 100g": "79mg", "% RDA": "\u2014", "Role": "Naturally occurring; not added"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Vitamin K", "Per 100g": "483\u00b5g", "% RDA": "402%", "Role": "Bone mineralisation; arterial calcium regulation; coagulation"}, {"Vitamin": "Folate", "Per 100g": "194\u00b5g", "% RDA": "49%", "Role": "DNA synthesis; methylation reactions; neural development"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin C", "Per 100g": "28.1mg", "% RDA": "31%", "Role": "Antioxidant; collagen synthesis; iron absorption enhancement"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin A (as beta-carotene)", "Per 100g": "469\u00b5g", "% RDA": "52\u201367%", "Role": "Cell signalling; immune function; vision"}, {"Vitamin": "Riboflavin (B2)", "Per 100g": "0.19mg", "% RDA": "15%", "Role": "Mitochondrial electron transport (FAD cofactor)"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin E", "Per 100g": "2.0mg", "% RDA": "13%", "Role": "Fat-soluble antioxidant; mitochondrial membrane protection"}]}, {"headers": ["Compound", "Per 100g", "Key Actions"], "rows": [{"Compound": "Lutein + Zeaxanthin", "Per 100g": "12,198\u00b5g", "Key Actions": "Concentrates in the macula; protects retinal cells from oxidative damage; highest concentration of any common food"}, {"Compound": "Beta-carotene", "Per 100g": "5,626\u00b5g", "Key Actions": "Provitamin A; antioxidant; anti-inflammatory"}, {"Compound": "Coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10)", "Per 100g": "trace\u20130.9mg", "Key Actions": "Direct mitochondrial electron transport cofactor; rare in plant foods; regenerates vitamin E"}, {"Compound": "Quercetin", "Per 100g": "~13mg", "Key Actions": "Anti-inflammatory; antiviral; mast cell stabiliser"}, {"Compound": "Kaempferol", "Per 100g": "~8mg", "Key Actions": "Anti-inflammatory; antiproliferative; heart protective"}, {"Compound": "Chlorophyll", "Per 100g": "high", "Key Actions": "Antioxidant; may bind dietary mutagens; liver protective"}]}, {"headers": ["Paired With", "Synergy", "Mechanism"], "rows": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Iron absorption + omega-3", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C in spinach enhances non-heme iron absorption; omega-3 from salmon reduces the inflammation that impairs iron utilisation"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Fat-soluble nutrient absorption", "Mechanism": "Egg fat acts as a carrier for spinach''s fat-soluble compounds (lutein, zeaxanthin, beta-carotene, vitamin K, vitamin E) \u2014 absorption increases substantially when fat is co-ingested"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Fat-soluble phytonutrient absorption", "Mechanism": "Same mechanism as eggs \u2014 avocado fat is an excellent carrier for spinach carotenoids; this is one of the most evidence-backed food pairings in the carotenoid bioavailability literature"}, {"Paired With": "Lemon juice", "Synergy": "Iron absorption", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C from lemon directly chelates non-heme iron, converting it to the ferrous form that absorbs 2\u20133\u00d7 more efficiently"}]}], "calories": "23", "protein": "2.9g", "carbs": "3.6g", "sugar": "0.4g", "fat": "0.4g", "sat._fat": "0.1g", "fibre": "2.2g", "cholesterol": "0mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Amount": "30g (side handful)", "Cal": "7", "Magnesium": "24mg", "Folate": "58\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "145\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "3,659\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "60g (large handful)", "Cal": "14", "Magnesium": "47mg", "Folate": "116\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "290\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "7,319\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "100g", "Cal": "23", "Magnesium": "79mg", "Folate": "194\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "483\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "12,198\u00b5g"}, {"Amount": "150g (cooked portion)", "Cal": "35", "Magnesium": "119mg", "Folate": "291\u00b5g", "Vitamin K": "725\u00b5g", "Lutein+Zeaxanthin": "18,297\u00b5g"}], "minerals_breakdown": [{"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "558mg", "% RDA": "12%", "Role": "Blood pressure regulation; muscle contraction; highest of common vegetables"}, {"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "79mg", "% RDA": "19%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; >300 enzymatic reactions; most people are deficient \u2014 spinach is one of the best dietary sources"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "2.7mg", "% RDA": "15\u201333%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain \u2014 non-heme iron; absorption improved substantially with vitamin C"}, {"Mineral": "Calcium", "Per 100g": "99mg", "% RDA": "10%", "Role": "Bone; muscle contraction \u2014 partially blocked by oxalates; cooking improves absorption"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "49mg", "% RDA": "4%", "Role": "ATP production"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "0.5mg", "% RDA": "5\u20136%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Sodium", "Per 100g": "79mg", "% RDA": "\u2014", "Role": "Naturally occurring; not added"}], "vitamins_breakdown": [{"Vitamin": "Vitamin K", "Per 100g": "483\u00b5g", "% RDA": "402%", "Role": "Bone mineralisation; arterial calcium regulation; coagulation"}, {"Vitamin": "Folate", "Per 100g": "194\u00b5g", "% RDA": "49%", "Role": "DNA synthesis; methylation reactions; neural development"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin C", "Per 100g": "28.1mg", "% RDA": "31%", "Role": "Antioxidant; collagen synthesis; iron absorption enhancement"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin A (as beta-carotene)", "Per 100g": "469\u00b5g", "% RDA": "52\u201367%", "Role": "Cell signalling; immune function; vision"}, {"Vitamin": "Riboflavin (B2)", "Per 100g": "0.19mg", "% RDA": "15%", "Role": "Mitochondrial electron transport (FAD cofactor)"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin E", "Per 100g": "2.0mg", "% RDA": "13%", "Role": "Fat-soluble antioxidant; mitochondrial membrane protection"}], "food_synergies": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Iron absorption + omega-3", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C in spinach enhances non-heme iron absorption; omega-3 from salmon reduces the inflammation that impairs iron utilisation"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]]", "Synergy": "Fat-soluble nutrient absorption", "Mechanism": "Egg fat acts as a carrier for spinach''s fat-soluble compounds (lutein, zeaxanthin, beta-carotene, vitamin K, vitamin E) \u2014 absorption increases substantially when fat is co-ingested"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]]", "Synergy": "Fat-soluble phytonutrient absorption", "Mechanism": "Same mechanism as eggs \u2014 avocado fat is an excellent carrier for spinach carotenoids; this is one of the most evidence-backed food pairings in the carotenoid bioavailability literature"}, {"Paired With": "Lemon juice", "Synergy": "Iron absorption", "Mechanism": "Vitamin C from lemon directly chelates non-heme iron, converting it to the ferrous form that absorbs 2\u20133\u00d7 more efficiently"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "vegetable", "calories_per_100g": "23", "protein_per_100g": "2.9", "carbs_per_100g": "3.6", "sugar_per_100g": "0.4", "added_sugar": "false", "fat_per_100g": "0.4", "saturated_fat_per_100g": "0.1", "fibre_per_100g": "2.2", "cholesterol_per_100g": "0", "omega3_per_100g_mg": "138", "iron_per_100g": "2.7", "zinc_per_100g": "0.5", "magnesium_per_100g": "79", "potassium_per_100g": "558", "sodium_per_100g": "79", "selenium_per_100g_\u00b5g": "1.0", "choline_per_100g_mg": "19", "vitamin_c_per_100g_mg": "28.1", "vitamin_d_per_100g_iu": "0", "vitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g": "0", "complete_protein": "false", "leucine_per_100g": "0.22", "antioxidant_level": "high", "probiotic": "false", "refined": "false", "seed_oil": "false", "body_systems": "", "serving_unit": "g", "serving_unit_g": "1", "typical_serving": "60g raw", "typical_serving_g": "60", "glycaemic_index": "15", "mitochondrial_benefit": "high", "callouts": [{"type": "success", "title": "Sports Nutrition Snapshot", "content": "**12,198\u00b5g lutein+zeaxanthin / 100g** \u00b7 **79mg magnesium / 100g** \u00b7 **GI: 15** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: High**\nThe most micronutrient-dense leafy green \u2014 and one of the richest plant sources of magnesium, the mineral most critical for ATP production."}, {"type": "quote", "title": "\"Dark leafy greens are the most nutrient-dense category of food on the planet \u2014 more vitamins, minerals, and phytonutrients per calorie than any other food group.\" \u2014 Casey Means", "content": ""}, {"type": "info", "title": "Volume loss when cooking", "content": "100g raw spinach \u2192 ~15g cooked (roughly 7:1 compression). A cooked spinach portion that looks small represents a large raw volume. Cooking reduces oxalates by ~30\u201350%, improving mineral absorption."}, {"type": "warning", "title": "Oxalates and iron absorption", "content": "Spinach contains oxalic acid, which binds to iron and calcium and reduces their absorption from spinach itself. This is not a reason to avoid spinach \u2014 the iron dose is still meaningful \u2014 but pairing spinach with **vitamin C** (lemon juice, blueberries) significantly improves non-heme iron absorption. Cooking reduces oxalate content by 30\u201350%."}, {"type": "info", "title": "CoQ10 in spinach", "content": "CoQ10 (ubiquinone) is a direct participant in the mitochondrial electron transport chain \u2014 it carries electrons between Complexes I/II and Complex III. Most dietary CoQ10 comes from organ meats and fatty fish; spinach is one of the few plant sources with measurable CoQ10 content."}, {"type": "info", "title": "How much and in what form?", "content": "**Optimal: 60\u2013150g raw daily** (or equivalent cooked \u2014 roughly 10\u201320g cooked)\n\nSpinach is effectively a free food from a calorie standpoint (23 cal/100g). There is no practical upper limit for healthy individuals without kidney issues.\n\n**Kidney stones / oxalates:** people with a history of calcium-oxalate kidney stones should moderate raw spinach intake. Cooking significantly reduces oxalate content \u2014 cooked spinach is preferable in this case.\n\n**Vitamin K and anticoagulants (warfarin):** very high vitamin K intake (483\u00b5g/100g) can interfere with warfarin dosing. Consistent intake (same amount daily) is preferable to large fluctuations.\n\n**Raw vs. cooked:** raw spinach preserves vitamin C and folate (heat-sensitive); cooked spinach reduces oxalates and makes lutein, zeaxanthin, and beta-carotene more bioavailable (heat breaks down cell walls). Both are valuable \u2014 variety is ideal."}], "quotes": ["\"Dark leafy greens are the most nutrient-dense category of food on the planet \u2014 more vitamins, minerals, and phytonutrients per calorie than any other food group.\" \u2014 Casey Means"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] How much and in what form?\n> **Optimal: 60\u2013150g raw daily** (or equivalent cooked \u2014 roughly 10\u201320g cooked)\n>\n> Spinach is effectively a free food from a calorie standpoint (23 cal/100g). There is no practical upper limit for healthy individuals without kidney issues.\n>\n> **Kidney stones / oxalates:** people with a history of calcium-oxalate kidney stones should moderate raw spinach intake. Cooking significantly reduces oxalate content \u2014 cooked spinach is preferable in this case.\n>\n> **Vitamin K and anticoagulants (warfarin):** very high vitamin K intake (483\u00b5g/100g) can interfere with warfarin dosing. Consistent intake (same amount daily) is preferable to large fluctuations.\n>\n> **Raw vs. cooked:** raw spinach preserves vitamin C and folate (heat-sensitive); cooked spinach reduces oxalates and makes lutein, zeaxanthin, and beta-carotene more bioavailable (heat breaks down cell walls). Both are valuable \u2014 variety is ideal.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "Raw in a salad with salmon, avocado, and lemon \u00b7 Wilted in olive oil as a side \u00b7 Blended into a smoothie (flavour is masked by fruit) \u00b7 Base layer under eggs\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Eggs]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Avocado]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Chicken Breast]]\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Spinach is one of the most micronutrient-dense foods available \u2014 the core of Means'' first trackable attribute. The magnesium content directly supports ATP production (the mitochondrial function target); the folate supports DNA methylation; the lutein and zeaxanthin protect mitochondrial membranes from oxidative damage. As a near-zero-calorie food with negligible glucose impact, it can be added to almost any meal without disrupting macronutrient targets.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 60g raw):**\n- Fibre: 1.3g of 50g target\n- Omega-3: 83mg of 2,000mg target (ALA, minimal)\n- Antioxidants: high (lutein/zeaxanthin, beta-carotene, quercetin, CoQ10)\n- Probiotics: none\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
