"rows": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "5mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "320mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin A", "Amount": "50mcg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin C", "Amount": "6mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin K", "Amount": "32mcg"}]}], "unit": "1 scoop", "calories": "150", "protein": "21g", "carbs": "12g", "sugar": "1g", "fat": "2.5g", "fiber": "5g", "cholesterol": "0mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Nutrient": "Calcium", "Amount": "75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Iron", "Amount": "5mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Potassium", "Amount": "75mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Sodium", "Amount": "320mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin A", "Amount": "50mcg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin C", "Amount": "6mg"}, {"Nutrient": "Vitamin K", "Amount": "32mcg"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "protein", "source": "notion", "notion_category": "Protein", "serving_unit": "1 scoop", "calories_per_unit": "150", "protein_per_unit_g": "21", "carbs_per_unit_g": "12", "fat_per_unit_g": "2.5", "fiber_per_unit_g": "5", "sugar_per_unit_g": "1", "cholesterol_per_unit_mg": "0", "processing_level": "Ultra-Processed", "fermented": "false", "callouts": [], "quotes": []}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;
insert into food_library (name, food_category, calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g, carbs_per_100g, fat_per_100g, fibre_per_100g, omega3_per_100g_mg, seed_oil, raw_payload) values ('Walnuts', 'nut', 654, 15.2, 13.7, 65.2, 6.7, 9080, false, '{"content_hash": "8c29c488afd7c66aa97566cb824011d4", "full_markdown_content": "---\ntags:\n  - nutrition/food\n  - nutrition/food/nut\nfood_category: nut\n# Macros per 100g\ncalories_per_100g: 654\nprotein_per_100g: 15.2\ncarbs_per_100g: 13.7\nsugar_per_100g: 2.6\nadded_sugar: false\nfat_per_100g: 65.2\nsaturated_fat_per_100g: 6.1\nfibre_per_100g: 6.7\ncholesterol_per_100g: 0\n# Omega-3\nomega3_per_100g_mg: 9080\n# Key minerals per 100g (mg unless noted)\niron_per_100g: 2.9\nzinc_per_100g: 3.1\nmagnesium_per_100g: 158\npotassium_per_100g: 441\nsodium_per_100g: 2\nselenium_per_100g_\u00b5g: 4.9\ncholine_per_100g_mg: 40\n# Key vitamins per 100g\nvitamin_c_per_100g_mg: 1.3\nvitamin_d_per_100g_iu: 0\nvitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g: 0\n# Protein quality\ncomplete_protein: false\nleucine_per_100g: 1.17\n# Good Energy attributes\nantioxidant_level: high\nprobiotic: false\nrefined: false\nseed_oil: false\n# Body systems\nbody_systems:\n  - brain\n  - heart\n  - gut\n  - mitochondria\n  - immune\n# Serving\nserving_unit: g\nserving_unit_g: 1\ntypical_serving: 30g (~15 halves)\ntypical_serving_g: 30\n# Classification\nglycaemic_index: 15\nmitochondrial_benefit: moderate\n---\n# Walnuts\n\n> [!success] Sports Nutrition Snapshot\n> **9,080mg ALA omega-3 / 100g** \u00b7 **Highest polyphenol content of any nut** \u00b7 **GI: 15** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: Moderate**\n> The only nut with a meaningful omega-3 contribution \u2014 and one of the richest dietary sources of brain-targeted polyphenols.\n\n> [!quote]\n> \"Walnuts are shaped like a brain for a reason \u2014 they contain more of the nutrients that support cognitive function than any other nut: ALA omega-3, polyphenols, melatonin, and folate.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick\n\nWalnuts are unique among nuts for their omega-3 content \u2014 9,080mg ALA per 100g, with an omega-6:omega-3 ratio of approximately 4:1 (far better than most other nuts and seed oils). They also have the highest polyphenol content of any nut, including ellagic acid (converted to urolithins by gut bacteria), melatonin (one of the few dietary sources), and a range of flavonoids with anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective properties.\n\n---\n\n## Macros\n\n### Per 100g\n\n| Calories | Protein | Carbs | Sugar | Fat | Sat. Fat | Fibre | Cholesterol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 654 | 15.2g | 13.7g | 2.6g | 65.2g | 6.1g | 6.7g | 0mg |\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n| Amount | Cal | Fat | Fibre | Omega-3 (ALA) | Magnesium |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 15g (~8 halves) | 98 | 9.8g | 1.0g | 1,362mg | 24mg |\n| 30g (~15 halves) | 196 | 19.6g | 2.0g | 2,724mg | 47mg |\n| 50g | 327 | 32.6g | 3.4g | 4,540mg | 79mg |\n\n> [!warning] Calorie density\n> At 654 cal/100g, walnuts are highly calorie-dense. A 30g serving (typical small handful) is ~196 calories \u2014 easily underestimated. Weigh rather than estimate by eye.\n\n---\n\n## Minerals\n\n| Mineral | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Magnesium | 158mg | 38% | ATP synthesis; muscle relaxation; cofactor for >300 enzymes |\n| Phosphorus | 346mg | 28% | ATP production; bone mineralisation |\n| Iron | 2.9mg | 16\u201336% | Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain |\n| Zinc | 3.1mg | 28\u201339% | Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function |\n| Potassium | 441mg | 9% | Electrolyte; blood pressure; muscle function |\n| Copper | 1.6mg | 178% | SOD1 antioxidant enzyme cofactor; iron metabolism \u2014 exceptional source |\n| Selenium | 4.9\u00b5g | 9% | Antioxidant defence |\n| Sodium | 2mg | \u2014 | Naturally negligible |\n\n---\n\n## Vitamins\n\n| Vitamin | Per 100g | % RDA | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Vitamin B6 | 0.54mg | 42% | Protein metabolism; neurotransmitter synthesis; glycogen breakdown |\n| Folate | 98\u00b5g | 25% | DNA synthesis; methylation; neural function |\n| Thiamine (B1) | 0.34mg | 28% | Carbohydrate-to-energy conversion |\n| Vitamin E | 0.7mg | 5% | Fat-soluble antioxidant (walnuts are lower than almonds in vitamin E but higher in polyphenols) |\n\n---\n\n## Fat and Polyphenol Profile\n\n### Fat Profile\n\n| Fat Type | Per 100g | Properties |\n|---|---|---|\n| ALA (omega-3) | ~9.1g | Plant-based omega-3; converts to EPA/DHA at ~5\u201310%; highest omega-3 of any nut |\n| Linoleic acid (omega-6) | ~38.1g | Omega-6:omega-3 ratio ~4:1 \u2014 the best ratio of any nut; still high in absolute terms |\n| Oleic acid (monounsaturated) | ~8.9g | Anti-inflammatory; stable |\n| Saturated fat | 6.1g | Moderate; not a significant concern at typical serving sizes |\n\n> [!warning] Omega-6 in context\n> Walnuts have the best omega-6:omega-3 ratio of any nut (~4:1), but the absolute omega-6 content (38g/100g) is high. At a 30g serving, this is ~11.4g omega-6 \u2014 significant. For people already consuming excess omega-6 from other sources, walnuts are still beneficial but portion control matters. For those eating a seed-oil-free diet, walnuts present no meaningful omega-6 concern at 30g/day.\n\n### Polyphenol Profile\n\n| Compound | Significance |\n|---|---|\n| Ellagic acid | Converted by gut bacteria to urolithins \u2014 compounds with anti-inflammatory, mitochondrial biogenesis, and muscle recovery effects only accessible via a healthy microbiome |\n| Juglone | Unique to walnuts; antimicrobial; anti-inflammatory |\n| Melatonin | One of the few dietary sources; supports circadian rhythm and sleep quality; also a potent mitochondrial antioxidant |\n| Quercetin | Anti-inflammatory; antiviral; mast cell stabiliser |\n| Catechins | Cardiovascular protective; antioxidant |\n\n> [!info] Urolithins and mitochondria\n> Urolithins (produced from ellagic acid by gut bacteria) have emerged as one of the more interesting compounds for mitochondrial health \u2014 they activate mitophagy (clearance of damaged mitochondria) and have been shown to improve mitochondrial function and muscle endurance in human trials. The conversion only occurs in people with the right gut microbiome composition \u2014 another reason gut health underpins metabolic outcomes.\n\n---\n\n## Food Synergies\n\n| Paired With | Synergy | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] | Urolithin production | Walnut ellagic acid is metabolised into urolithins by the gut bacteria present in Greek yogurt; eating walnuts alongside a probiotic source maximises the chance of complete conversion |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] | Dual polyphenol synergy | Walnut polyphenols (ellagic acid, catechins) + blueberry anthocyanins act through complementary anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective pathways; combined, they represent the most polyphenol-dense snack combination in the vault |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] | Satiety + glucose management | Walnut fat dramatically slows gastric emptying; adding walnuts to oats reduces the glucose spike and extends satiety well past the meal |\n| [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]] | Omega-3 complementarity | ALA (walnuts) + EPA/DHA (salmon) \u2014 plant and marine omega-3 together provide broader metabolic coverage than either alone |\n\n---\n\n## Dosage Guidance\n\n> [!info] Optimal portion and timing\n> **Optimal: 28\u201330g per day (~15 walnut halves)**\n> - 30g provides ~2.7g ALA omega-3, 2g fibre, and a meaningful polyphenol dose\n> - The majority of clinical benefit studies used 28\u201342g/day\n> - Above 60g/day, the high calorie density (654 cal/100g) and absolute omega-6 content become meaningful constraints\n>\n> **Timing:** walnuts contain melatonin \u2014 eating them as an evening snack may subtly support sleep onset. There is no strong evidence for a specific optimal timing for the other benefits.\n>\n> **Raw vs. roasted:** raw or lightly roasted walnuts preserve polyphenol content better than heavily roasted. Avoid walnuts roasted in seed oils or salted. Dry-roasted or raw are the best options.\n>\n> **Storage:** walnuts go rancid quickly (high PUFA content oxidises at room temperature). Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator or freezer; use within 3 months of opening.\n\n---\n\n## Good Energy Relevance\n\nWalnuts are the most polyphenol-rich nut \u2014 and the only nut with a meaningful omega-3 contribution. They directly address three of Means'' five trackable attributes: omega-3 (ALA), antioxidants (ellagic acid, catechins, melatonin), and micronutrient density (magnesium, copper, B6, folate). The ellagic acid \u2192 urolithin pathway connects walnut consumption to both gut microbiome health and mitochondrial biogenesis \u2014 a rare dual benefit.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 30g):**\n- Fibre: 2.0g of 50g target (4%)\n- Omega-3: 2,724mg ALA (converts to ~136\u2013272mg EPA/DHA)\n- Antioxidants: high (ellagic acid, catechins, melatonin, quercetin)\n- Probiotics: none (but ellagic acid feeds probiotic bacteria)\n\n---\n\n## Meal Ideas\n\nSmall handful as a snack with blueberries \u00b7 Stirred into Greek yogurt \u00b7 Added to a salad \u00b7 As an evening snack (melatonin content supports circadian timing)\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]\n\n---\n\n## From Notion (unit-based reference)\n\nNotion''s Foods database classified Walnuts as **Processing Level: Whole/Unprocessed**, **Fermented: No** \u2014 consistent with this file. Its serving reference was 1 cup (100g) = 750 cal / 14g protein / 12g carbs / 70g fat / 6g fibre / 80mg calcium / 5.8mg iron / 400mg potassium \u2014 close to the Per 100g figures above (minor variance likely reflects a different source dataset, not a different product).\n", "portion_state": "raw_dry", "tables": [{"headers": ["Calories", "Protein", "Carbs", "Sugar", "Fat", "Sat. Fat", "Fibre", "Cholesterol"], "rows": [{"Calories": "654", "Protein": "15.2g", "Carbs": "13.7g", "Sugar": "2.6g", "Fat": "65.2g", "Sat. Fat": "6.1g", "Fibre": "6.7g", "Cholesterol": "0mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Amount", "Cal", "Fat", "Fibre", "Omega-3 (ALA)", "Magnesium"], "rows": [{"Amount": "15g (~8 halves)", "Cal": "98", "Fat": "9.8g", "Fibre": "1.0g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "1,362mg", "Magnesium": "24mg"}, {"Amount": "30g (~15 halves)", "Cal": "196", "Fat": "19.6g", "Fibre": "2.0g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "2,724mg", "Magnesium": "47mg"}, {"Amount": "50g", "Cal": "327", "Fat": "32.6g", "Fibre": "3.4g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "4,540mg", "Magnesium": "79mg"}]}, {"headers": ["Mineral", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "158mg", "% RDA": "38%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; muscle relaxation; cofactor for >300 enzymes"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "346mg", "% RDA": "28%", "Role": "ATP production; bone mineralisation"}, {"Mineral": "Iron", "Per 100g": "2.9mg", "% RDA": "16\u201336%", "Role": "Oxygen transport; mitochondrial electron chain"}, {"Mineral": "Zinc", "Per 100g": "3.1mg", "% RDA": "28\u201339%", "Role": "Protein synthesis; testosterone; immune function"}, {"Mineral": "Potassium", "Per 100g": "441mg", "% RDA": "9%", "Role": "Electrolyte; blood pressure; muscle function"}, {"Mineral": "Copper", "Per 100g": "1.6mg", "% RDA": "178%", "Role": "SOD1 antioxidant enzyme cofactor; iron metabolism \u2014 exceptional source"}, {"Mineral": "Selenium", "Per 100g": "4.9\u00b5g", "% RDA": "9%", "Role": "Antioxidant defence"}, {"Mineral": "Sodium", "Per 100g": "2mg", "% RDA": "\u2014", "Role": "Naturally negligible"}]}, {"headers": ["Vitamin", "Per 100g", "% RDA", "Role"], "rows": [{"Vitamin": "Vitamin B6", "Per 100g": "0.54mg", "% RDA": "42%", "Role": "Protein metabolism; neurotransmitter synthesis; glycogen breakdown"}, {"Vitamin": "Folate", "Per 100g": "98\u00b5g", "% RDA": "25%", "Role": "DNA synthesis; methylation; neural function"}, {"Vitamin": "Thiamine (B1)", "Per 100g": "0.34mg", "% RDA": "28%", "Role": "Carbohydrate-to-energy conversion"}, {"Vitamin": "Vitamin E", "Per 100g": "0.7mg", "% RDA": "5%", "Role": "Fat-soluble antioxidant (walnuts are lower than almonds in vitamin E but higher in polyphenols)"}]}, {"headers": ["Fat Type", "Per 100g", "Properties"], "rows": [{"Fat Type": "ALA (omega-3)", "Per 100g": "~9.1g", "Properties": "Plant-based omega-3; converts to EPA/DHA at ~5\u201310%; highest omega-3 of any nut"}, {"Fat Type": "Linoleic acid (omega-6)", "Per 100g": "~38.1g", "Properties": "Omega-6:omega-3 ratio ~4:1 \u2014 the best ratio of any nut; still high in absolute terms"}, {"Fat Type": "Oleic acid (monounsaturated)", "Per 100g": "~8.9g", "Properties": "Anti-inflammatory; stable"}, {"Fat Type": "Saturated fat", "Per 100g": "6.1g", "Properties": "Moderate; not a significant concern at typical serving sizes"}]}, {"headers": ["Compound", "Significance"], "rows": [{"Compound": "Ellagic acid", "Significance": "Converted by gut bacteria to urolithins \u2014 compounds with anti-inflammatory, mitochondrial biogenesis, and muscle recovery effects only accessible via a healthy microbiome"}, {"Compound": "Juglone", "Significance": "Unique to walnuts; antimicrobial; anti-inflammatory"}, {"Compound": "Melatonin", "Significance": "One of the few dietary sources; supports circadian rhythm and sleep quality; also a potent mitochondrial antioxidant"}, {"Compound": "Quercetin", "Significance": "Anti-inflammatory; antiviral; mast cell stabiliser"}, {"Compound": "Catechins", "Significance": "Cardiovascular protective; antioxidant"}]}, {"headers": ["Paired With", "Synergy", "Mechanism"], "rows": [{"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]]", "Synergy": "Urolithin production", "Mechanism": "Walnut ellagic acid is metabolised into urolithins by the gut bacteria present in Greek yogurt; eating walnuts alongside a probiotic source maximises the chance of complete conversion"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]]", "Synergy": "Dual polyphenol synergy", "Mechanism": "Walnut polyphenols (ellagic acid, catechins) + blueberry anthocyanins act through complementary anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective pathways; combined, they represent the most polyphenol-dense snack combination in the vault"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]", "Synergy": "Satiety + glucose management", "Mechanism": "Walnut fat dramatically slows gastric emptying; adding walnuts to oats reduces the glucose spike and extends satiety well past the meal"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Omega-3 complementarity", "Mechanism": "ALA (walnuts) + EPA/DHA (salmon) \u2014 plant and marine omega-3 together provide broader metabolic coverage than either alone"}]}], "calories": "654", "protein": "15.2g", "carbs": "13.7g", "sugar": "2.6g", "fat": "65.2g", "sat._fat": "6.1g", "fibre": "6.7g", "cholesterol": "0mg", "quick_reference_servings": [{"Amount": "15g (~8 halves)", "Cal": "98", "Fat": "9.8g", "Fibre": "1.0g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "1,362mg", "Magnesium": "24mg"}, {"Amount": "30g (~15 halves)", "Cal": "196", "Fat": "19.6g", "Fibre": "2.0g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "2,724mg", "Magnesium": "47mg"}, {"Amount": "50g", "Cal": "327", "Fat": "32.6g", "Fibre": "3.4g", "Omega-3 (ALA)": "4,540mg", "Magnesium": "79mg"}], "minerals_breakdown": [{"Mineral": "Magnesium", "Per 100g": "158mg", "% RDA": "38%", "Role": "ATP synthesis; muscle relaxation; cofactor for >300 enzymes"}, {"Mineral": "Phosphorus", "Per 100g": "346mg", "% RDA": "28%", "Role": "ATP production; bone m