ult"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]]", "Synergy": "Satiety + glucose management", "Mechanism": "Walnut fat dramatically slows gastric emptying; adding walnuts to oats reduces the glucose spike and extends satiety well past the meal"}, {"Paired With": "[[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]", "Synergy": "Omega-3 complementarity", "Mechanism": "ALA (walnuts) + EPA/DHA (salmon) \u2014 plant and marine omega-3 together provide broader metabolic coverage than either alone"}], "tags": "", "food_category": "nut", "calories_per_100g": "654", "protein_per_100g": "15.2", "carbs_per_100g": "13.7", "sugar_per_100g": "2.6", "added_sugar": "false", "fat_per_100g": "65.2", "saturated_fat_per_100g": "6.1", "fibre_per_100g": "6.7", "cholesterol_per_100g": "0", "omega3_per_100g_mg": "9080", "iron_per_100g": "2.9", "zinc_per_100g": "3.1", "magnesium_per_100g": "158", "potassium_per_100g": "441", "sodium_per_100g": "2", "selenium_per_100g_\u00b5g": "4.9", "choline_per_100g_mg": "40", "vitamin_c_per_100g_mg": "1.3", "vitamin_d_per_100g_iu": "0", "vitamin_b12_per_100g_\u00b5g": "0", "complete_protein": "false", "leucine_per_100g": "1.17", "antioxidant_level": "high", "probiotic": "false", "refined": "false", "seed_oil": "false", "body_systems": "", "serving_unit": "g", "serving_unit_g": "1", "typical_serving": "30g (~15 halves)", "typical_serving_g": "30", "glycaemic_index": "15", "mitochondrial_benefit": "moderate", "callouts": [{"type": "success", "title": "Sports Nutrition Snapshot", "content": "**9,080mg ALA omega-3 / 100g** \u00b7 **Highest polyphenol content of any nut** \u00b7 **GI: 15** \u00b7 **Mitochondrial benefit: Moderate**\nThe only nut with a meaningful omega-3 contribution \u2014 and one of the richest dietary sources of brain-targeted polyphenols."}, {"type": "quote", "title": "\"Walnuts are shaped like a brain for a reason \u2014 they contain more of the nutrients that support cognitive function than any other nut: ALA omega-3, polyphenols, melatonin, and folate.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick", "content": ""}, {"type": "warning", "title": "Calorie density", "content": "At 654 cal/100g, walnuts are highly calorie-dense. A 30g serving (typical small handful) is ~196 calories \u2014 easily underestimated. Weigh rather than estimate by eye."}, {"type": "warning", "title": "Omega-6 in context", "content": "Walnuts have the best omega-6:omega-3 ratio of any nut (~4:1), but the absolute omega-6 content (38g/100g) is high. At a 30g serving, this is ~11.4g omega-6 \u2014 significant. For people already consuming excess omega-6 from other sources, walnuts are still beneficial but portion control matters. For those eating a seed-oil-free diet, walnuts present no meaningful omega-6 concern at 30g/day."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Urolithins and mitochondria", "content": "Urolithins (produced from ellagic acid by gut bacteria) have emerged as one of the more interesting compounds for mitochondrial health \u2014 they activate mitophagy (clearance of damaged mitochondria) and have been shown to improve mitochondrial function and muscle endurance in human trials. The conversion only occurs in people with the right gut microbiome composition \u2014 another reason gut health underpins metabolic outcomes."}, {"type": "info", "title": "Optimal portion and timing", "content": "**Optimal: 28\u201330g per day (~15 walnut halves)**\n- 30g provides ~2.7g ALA omega-3, 2g fibre, and a meaningful polyphenol dose\n- The majority of clinical benefit studies used 28\u201342g/day\n- Above 60g/day, the high calorie density (654 cal/100g) and absolute omega-6 content become meaningful constraints\n\n**Timing:** walnuts contain melatonin \u2014 eating them as an evening snack may subtly support sleep onset. There is no strong evidence for a specific optimal timing for the other benefits.\n\n**Raw vs. roasted:** raw or lightly roasted walnuts preserve polyphenol content better than heavily roasted. Avoid walnuts roasted in seed oils or salted. Dry-roasted or raw are the best options.\n\n**Storage:** walnuts go rancid quickly (high PUFA content oxidises at room temperature). Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator or freezer; use within 3 months of opening."}], "quotes": ["\"Walnuts are shaped like a brain for a reason \u2014 they contain more of the nutrients that support cognitive function than any other nut: ALA omega-3, polyphenols, melatonin, and folate.\" \u2014 Rhonda Patrick"], "dosage_and_timing_guidance": "> [!info] Optimal portion and timing\n> **Optimal: 28\u201330g per day (~15 walnut halves)**\n> - 30g provides ~2.7g ALA omega-3, 2g fibre, and a meaningful polyphenol dose\n> - The majority of clinical benefit studies used 28\u201342g/day\n> - Above 60g/day, the high calorie density (654 cal/100g) and absolute omega-6 content become meaningful constraints\n>\n> **Timing:** walnuts contain melatonin \u2014 eating them as an evening snack may subtly support sleep onset. There is no strong evidence for a specific optimal timing for the other benefits.\n>\n> **Raw vs. roasted:** raw or lightly roasted walnuts preserve polyphenol content better than heavily roasted. Avoid walnuts roasted in seed oils or salted. Dry-roasted or raw are the best options.\n>\n> **Storage:** walnuts go rancid quickly (high PUFA content oxidises at room temperature). Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator or freezer; use within 3 months of opening.\n\n---", "meal_ideas": "Small handful as a snack with blueberries \u00b7 Stirred into Greek yogurt \u00b7 Added to a salad \u00b7 As an evening snack (melatonin content supports circadian timing)\n\n**Pairs well with:** [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Blueberries]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Greek Yogurt]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Oats]] \u00b7 [[\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Control Center/Supabase Data/Foods/Salmon]]\n\n---", "good_energy_relevance": "Walnuts are the most polyphenol-rich nut \u2014 and the only nut with a meaningful omega-3 contribution. They directly address three of Means'' five trackable attributes: omega-3 (ALA), antioxidants (ellagic acid, catechins, melatonin), and micronutrient density (magnesium, copper, B6, folate). The ellagic acid \u2192 urolithin pathway connects walnut consumption to both gut microbiome health and mitochondrial biogenesis \u2014 a rare dual benefit.\n\n**Good Energy daily targets contribution (per 30g):**\n- Fibre: 2.0g of 50g target (4%)\n- Omega-3: 2,724mg ALA (converts to ~136\u2013272mg EPA/DHA)\n- Antioxidants: high (ellagic acid, catechins, melatonin, quercetin)\n- Probiotics: none (but ellagic acid feeds probiotic bacteria)\n\n---"}'::jsonb) on conflict (name) do update set calories_per_100g = excluded.calories_per_100g, protein_per_100g = excluded.protein_per_100g, raw_payload = excluded.raw_payload;